window.HANDBOOK = [{"n":1,"name":"Networking & Low-Level I/O","cases":[{"slug":"raw-tcp-cdp-handshake","q":"How to query CDP endpoints over raw TCP bypassing Chromium Private Network Access (PNA) and CORS without HTTP client bloat?","problem":"When building sovereign desktop harnesses or local orchestration runtimes that drive Chrome via the Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP on port `9222`), querying target endpoints (`http://127.0.0.1:9222/json`) from an embedded WebView is strictly blocked by **Chromium Private Network Access (PNA)** and **CORS**.\n\nTo circumvent this, the desktop host process (Rust / OS layer) must perform the local target discovery.","naive":[{"t":"Heavy HTTP Client Crates (`reqwest`, `hyper`, `surf`)","n":["Pulling in `reqwest` pulls in an async runtime, connection pool, and TLS stack (such as `native-tls` or `rustls`).","Increases release binary size by adding HTTP client and async/TLS runtime dependencies.","Introduces non-trivial initialization latency for what is fundamentally an ephemeral localhost probe."]},{"t":"Local Proxy Daemons","n":["Running a background proxy process introduces port contention, process-monitoring overhead, and OS firewall prompts."]},{"t":"Socket EOF Block","n":["Many naive socket implementations read until `EOF` (0 bytes). Because HTTP/1.1 connections remain open unless explicitly closed or keep-alive expires, read loops hang until socket timeout unless `Content-Length` framing is actively parsed."]}],"arch":"We eliminated external HTTP dependencies entirely by implementing a handcrafted raw TCP HTTP/1.1 handshake using Rust's `std::net::TcpStream`:\n\n1. **Direct Socket Connection:** `TcpStream::connect_timeout` connects to `127.0.0.1:9222` with an explicit timeout.\n2. **Handcrafted Frame:** A statically formatted minimal HTTP/1.1 request is written directly to the stream:\n   ```http\n   GET /json HTTP/1.1\\r\\n\n   Host: 127.0.0.1:9222\\r\\n\n   Connection: close\\r\\n\\r\\n\n   ```\n3. **Active Framing & Early Exit:** Rather than waiting for the remote socket to close or timeout, the buffer is inspected in-flight for the `\\r\\n\\r\\n` header delimiter and the `Content-Length` header. As soon as the byte count of the body meets or exceeds `Content-Length`, execution returns immediately.","verify":"1. Start Google Chrome with remote debugging enabled on port 9222:\n   ```bash\n   chrome.exe --remote-debugging-port=9222 --headless --disable-gpu about:blank\n   ```\n\n2. Execute the Rust client to perform the raw TCP HTTP/1.1 handshake and Content-Length parse:\n   ```bash\n   cargo run --release\n   ```\n\nThe application connects to `127.0.0.1:9222`, transmits the handcrafted HTTP/1.1 frame, parses `Content-Length`, exits immediately upon frame completion, and outputs the elapsed handshake duration.","src":"src/main.rs"},{"slug":"client-disconnect-socket-abort-cascade","q":"How to cascade client TCP disconnects to immediately splice queued tasks and abort long-running Chromium workers?","problem":"In high-overhead audit systems (running Chromium, ZAP security scans, and Lighthouse), audits can take anywhere from 10 to 60 seconds. When users close the browser tab, refresh the page, or lose network connectivity mid-scan, Node.js HTTP servers keep executing the downstream asynchronous tasks by default, wasting significant CPU and RAM on results that will never be delivered.","naive":[{"t":"Default Express/Node.js Request Lifecycle","n":["When an HTTP socket closes, Node.js continues executing existing `async/await` promises. It only fails when the handler eventually tries to call `res.json()`."]},{"t":"Orphaned Chromium Subprocesses","n":["Headless browsers continue loading heavy web pages, rendering DOM trees, and executing JavaScript long after the client has disconnected."]},{"t":"Queue Starvation for Active Users","n":["Disconnected requests waiting in concurrency queues still execute when their turn arrives, starving active users of worker capacity."]}],"arch":"1. **Socket Event Binding:** `requestController()` hooks both `request.once('aborted')` and `response.once('close')`. If the client closes the TCP socket before `writableEnded`, it aborts the controller with HTTP 499 `CLIENT_DISCONNECTED`.\n2. **Queue-Level Splicing:** In `TaskPool`, each queued job registers a one-shot abort listener. If a client disconnects while waiting in line, `this.queue.splice(index, 1)` removes the job immediately, freeing worker slots for active users.\n3. **Cascading Child Process Abort:** Active workers receive the cascading `controller.signal`. Headless browser wrappers listen to this signal and kill Chromium child processes via `SIGKILL`, reclaiming memory within milliseconds.\n4. **Explicit Cleanup:** Unbinds socket listeners in `cleanup()` upon successful completion to prevent memory leaks.\n\n```javascript\n// 1. Hooking TCP socket abort and response close to AbortController\nfunction requestController(request, response) {\n    const controller = new AbortController();\n    const cancel = () => {\n        if (!response.writableEnded && !controller.signal.aborted) {\n            controller.abort(new AppError('The client disconnected.', {\n                status: 499,\n                code: 'CLIENT_DISCONNECTED'\n            }));\n        }\n    };\n    request.once('aborted', cancel);\n    response.once('close', cancel);\n    return {\n        signal: controller.signal,\n        cleanup() {\n            request.off('aborted', cancel);\n            response.off('close', cancel);\n        }\n    };\n}\n\n// 2. Splicing queued jobs immediately upon client disconnection\njob.onAbort = () => {\n    const index = this.queue.indexOf(job);\n    if (index >= 0) this.queue.splice(index, 1);\n    reject(signal.reason || this.abortError());\n};\nsignal?.addEventListener('abort', job.onAbort, { once: true });\n```","verify":"","src":"src/socket_abort_cascade.js"},{"slug":"nginx-sse-nobuffer-spa-mime-confusion-defense","q":"How to eliminate Nginx SSE streaming buffer latency and prevent SPA MIME-type confusion on missing assets?","problem":"In modern web architectures combining Single Page Applications (React/Vite) with streaming backend APIs (Server-Sent Events / SSE), two major reverse proxy bugs commonly occur in production:\n1. **Nginx Buffers SSE Streams:** By default, Nginx buffers upstream responses in memory chunks, causing live audit progress packets to stall and burst all at once or time out with 504 Gateway Timeout.\n2. **SPA Routing Triggers MIME-Type Confusion:** Naive SPA configurations use `try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html`. When a browser requests a deleted or missing hashed JavaScript bundle (`/assets/chunk-abc123.js`), Nginx returns `200 OK` with `index.html`. The browser attempts to execute the HTML document as JavaScript and crashes with `Uncaught SyntaxError: Unexpected token '<'`.","naive":[{"t":"Default Nginx Proxy Buffering (`proxy_buffering on`)","n":["Nginx holds upstream bytes until the buffer size (`proxy_buffer_size`) is filled. Real-time SSE micro-updates never reach client browsers in real time."]},{"t":"Default Short Proxy Timeouts (`60s`)","n":["Multi-stage automated audits take 90-180 seconds. Default 60-second read timeouts abruptly drop persistent SSE TCP connections."]},{"t":"Catch-All SPA Routing Fallback","n":["Applying `try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html` globally across the entire domain treats missing scripts, stylesheets, and images as virtual SPA routes, breaking client caches and polluting error logs."]}],"arch":"1. **Explicit Buffering Disablement:** Enforces `proxy_buffering off;` on the `/api/` reverse proxy block, guaranteeing immediate TCP packet flushing for live SSE streams.\n2. **Extended Audit Timeouts:** Configures `proxy_read_timeout 270s;` and `proxy_send_timeout 270s;` to accommodate long-running security audits without premature proxy disconnection.\n3. **Isolated Static Asset Block:** Dedicated location block for static extensions (`.(?:js|css|svg|png|webp|woff2?)$`) configured with `try_files $uri =404;`. If a hashed asset does not exist on disk, Nginx returns an explicit HTTP 404 rather than serving `index.html`, eliminating MIME-type confusion crashes.\n4. **Long-Term Asset Caching:** Configures `expires 1y;` on immutable static bundles for optimal browser cache performance.\n\n```nginx\n# 1. Real-time Server-Sent Events (SSE) streaming proxy configuration\nlocation /api/ {\n    proxy_pass http://backend:5000;\n    proxy_http_version 1.1;\n    proxy_set_header Host $host;\n    proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;\n    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;\n    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;\n    proxy_connect_timeout 10s;\n    proxy_read_timeout 270s;\n    proxy_send_timeout 270s;\n    proxy_buffering off;\n}\n\n# 2. SPA index.html fallback for client routing\nlocation / { try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html; }\n\n# 3. Static asset defense preventing HTML fallback on 404 script/style requests\nlocation ~* \\.(?:js|css|svg|png|jpg|jpeg|gif|webp|woff2?)$ {\n    expires 1y;\n    try_files $uri =404;\n}\n```","verify":"","src":"src/nginx_sse_spa.conf"},{"slug":"debounced-webrtc-orphan-session-teardown","q":"How to eliminate orphan WebRTC audio channels without dropping calls during mobile Wi-Fi to 5G handovers?","problem":"1. **Zombie Media Server Channels:** In peer-to-peer or SFU WebRTC architectures (LiveKit, Agora), users frequently forget to hang up after the other party leaves. The room stays open indefinitely, burning TURN bandwidth and CPU.\n2. **Premature Disconnection on Network Flaps:** When mobile users switch between Wi-Fi and 5G cellular, WebRTC renegotiation briefly drops the participant count to 1. An immediate teardown kills the call right before the user reconnects.\n3. **Unbilled Session Inaccuracies:** Audio and video calls require accurate start-to-finish duration logging in PostgreSQL audit ledgers without manual user action.","naive":[{"t":"Immediate `count === 1` Exit","n":["Terminating the call immediately upon peer departure destroys user experience during brief packet loss or ICE renegotiation."]},{"t":"Missing State Latches","n":["Triggering teardown when `count === 1` at room creation disconnects the first user before the invited peer has had time to accept the call."]},{"t":"Server-Side Polling Daemons","n":["Polling WebRTC room APIs from cron workers introduces 30-60s latency, during which video streams continue burning bandwidth."]}],"arch":"1. **Multi-Participant Latch (`hasHadMultiple`):** Teardown logic only activates once the room has verified at least 2 active participants (`count >= 2`), allowing the room initiator to wait without premature disconnection.\n2. **Debounced Flap Guard (`debounceMs = 2000`):** When participant count drops from 2+ to 1, a 2000ms timer starts. If the peer reconnects within 2 seconds, the timer is cleared immediately.\n3. **Atomic Session Settlement:** Upon timeout expiration, local audio/video media tracks are released, the room status is marked `ended` in PostgreSQL, and the session duration is durably logged.","verify":"","src":"src/webrtc_teardown_guard.ts"},{"slug":"literal-escaped-parameter-route-compiler","q":"How to safely compile dynamic parameter routes with literal metacharacter escaping and reactive signal resolution?","problem":"Client-side Single Page Application routers that support file-system-style parameter routes (such as Next.js/SvelteKit `/users/[userId]/posts/[postId]`) compile paths into regular expressions. If static path segments contain literal regex characters—such as version dots (`/docs/v1.0/[slug]`) or parentheses (`/releases/(draft)/[id]`)—naive string substitution produces unescaped regular expressions, causing wildcard injection, unintended matches, or syntax crashes.","naive":[{"t":"Unescaped Dot Wildcards","n":["`/docs/v1.0/[slug]` compiles to `/docs/v1.0/([^/]+)`. Because `.` is an unescaped regex wildcard, `/docs/v1x0/slug` falsely matches!"]},{"t":"Parentheses Capture Group Corruption","n":["Literal parentheses `/(draft)/` alter regex capture group numbering, shifting parameter extraction indices."]},{"t":"Query String Contamination","n":["Matching raw `window.location.href` without pathname isolation causes query parameters (`?preview=true`) to pollute dynamic route parameters."]}],"arch":"1. **Metacharacter Escaping Pass:** `escapeRegex()` replaces all special characters (`.*+?^${}()|[]\\`) with escaped literals (`\\\\`).\n2. **Bracket Parameter Tokenization:** Scans route paths for bracket tokens (`/[([^]]+)]/g`). Each token compiles into `([^/]+)` while its identifier is pushed to `paramNames`.\n3. **URL Pathname Isolation:** Normalizes URLs via `new URL(url, 'http://localhost').pathname`, cleanly stripping query strings and hash anchors before route evaluation.\n4. **Reactive Navigation Signals:** Successfully matched routes update `currentPath` and `currentParams` signals, waking only subscribed UI components.\n\n```typescript\n// 1. Literal regex metacharacter escaping pass\nfunction escapeRegex(value: string): string {\n  return value.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\\]\\\\]/g, '\\\\$&');\n}\n\n// 2. Compiling [param] paths into safe regular expressions\nfunction compileRoutePath(path: string): { regex: RegExp; paramNames: string[] } {\n  const paramNames: string[] = [];\n  const parameterPattern = /\\[([^\\]]+)\\]/g;\n  let regexPath = '';\n  let cursor = 0;\n  let match: RegExpExecArray | null;\n\n  while ((match = parameterPattern.exec(path)) !== null) {\n    regexPath += escapeRegex(path.slice(cursor, match.index));\n    regexPath += '([^/]+)';\n    paramNames.push(match[1]);\n    cursor = match.index + match[0].length;\n  }\n\n  regexPath += escapeRegex(path.slice(cursor));\n  return { regex: new RegExp(`^${regexPath}$`), paramNames };\n}\n```","verify":"Run the automated test suite using Node.js built-in test runner with native TypeScript type stripping (requires Node 22.6+):\n\n```bash\nnode --experimental-strip-types --test test/route_compiler.test.ts\n```\n\n### Verified Output:\n```text\n✔ route with dot literal (/v1.0/[id]) matches exact dot and rejects wildcard matches (/v1x0/[id]) (1.54ms)\n✔ route with parenthesis literal (/(draft)/[slug]) treats parentheses literally without capturing groups (0.27ms)\n✔ escapeRegex escapes all standard regex metacharacters (0.19ms)\nℹ tests 3\nℹ suites 0\nℹ pass 3\nℹ fail 0\n```\n\n* **Test Environment:** Node.js v24.17.0, Windows 11, 2026-10-17 (no external dependencies, native type stripping)*","src":"src/route_compiler.ts"}],"dir":"01-networking-and-io","blurb":"Socket framing, protocol engines, raw I/O"},{"n":2,"name":"Security & Sandboxing","cases":[{"slug":"mid-flight-mutating-request-guard","q":"How to prevent autonomous AI browser agents from executing destructive HTTP state mutations (POST, DELETE, PUT) in mid-flight?","problem":"Autonomous AI browser agents executing tasks on behalf of a user possess authenticated session access to services like GitHub, AWS, cloud providers, and banking portals.\n\nIf an LLM hallucinates an interaction or follows a prompt injection embedded in page HTML, it can dispatch mutating HTTP requests (`POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`, `PATCH`) that permanently delete repositories, alter DNS, or trigger financial transfers.","naive":[{"t":"Pre-Click DOM Confirmation Prompts","n":["Asking a human \"May I click this button?\" fails because DOM text labels are unreliable. A button labeled \"Continue\" or \"Next\" can trigger a background `DELETE` or `POST` mutation via dynamic JavaScript event listeners."]},{"t":"Post-Request Audit Logs","n":["Logging requests after execution is too late; once TCP frames leave the network interface, irreversible mutations have already executed on the remote server."]},{"t":"Local Forwarding Proxies (mitmproxy, Squid)","n":["Heavyweight, requires installing local root CA certificates for TLS decryption, and breaks on HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 without extensive configuration."]}],"arch":"We hooked directly into Chrome DevTools Protocol's low-level `Fetch` domain (`Fetch.enable` with request-stage interception):\n\n1. **In-Flight Suspension:** Before an HTTP request frame leaves the browser process, Chrome fires `Fetch.requestPaused`.\n2. **Verb Filter:** Safe read-only requests (`GET`, `HEAD`, `OPTIONS`) are immediately forwarded via `Fetch.continueRequest`.\n3. **Interactive Human Sign-Off:** Mutating requests (`POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`, `PATCH`) are held in memory. The desktop UI presents the destination URL, method, and payload to the user.\n4. **Deterministic Gate:** The request is only permitted onto the network wire upon explicit human approval.","verify":"","src":"src/guard.ts"},{"slug":"socket-level-ssrf-dns-rebinding-defense","q":"How to prevent SSRF and DNS Rebinding (TOCTOU) attacks at the socket transport layer without reverse proxy daemons?","problem":"Visiting user-submitted URLs in browser workers creates catastrophic SSRF vulnerability against cloud metadata endpoints (169.254.169.254), container localhost, and RFC 1918 LANs.\n\nStandard DNS pre-validation fails against DNS Rebinding (Time-of-Check to Time-of-Use): the DNS resolver returns a benign public IP at validation time, but by the time the socket connects shortly after, the short-TTL DNS switches to 127.0.0.1.","naive":[{"t":"Regex & URL Hostname Whitelisting","n":["Trivial to bypass with decimal/octal encodings (`http://2130706433`), IPv6 mapped IPv4 (`::ffff:127.0.0.1`), or embedded credentials (`user:pass@evil.com`)."]},{"t":"Pre-flight DNS Resolvers without Socket Pinning","n":["Validating an IP before making a request creates a Time-of-Check to Time-of-Use (TOCTOU) vulnerability. Malicious DNS servers return a public IP for the check, then immediately respond with `127.0.0.1` for the fetch."]},{"t":"Heavyweight Reverse Proxy Daemons (Squid / HAProxy)","n":["Adds operational overhead, complex routing topologies, and separate certificate lifecycle maintenance."]}],"arch":"1. **Microsecond Subnet Classification:** Using Node.js `net.BlockList()` to block all private, loopback, link-local, multicast, and CGNAT subnets for IPv4 and IPv6.\n2. **Socket-Level IP Pinning:** Upstream `net.connect()` and `https.request()` connect directly to the resolved and verified IP address (`target.address`). The original hostname is supplied strictly as the TLS SNI (`servername`) and HTTP `Host` header.\n3. **Fail-Closed CONNECT Policy:** In read-only passive scanning mode, opaque HTTP `CONNECT` tunnels are rejected with HTTP 405 because method payloads cannot be inspected inside the tunnel.\n4. **Streaming Chunk Quotas:** In-flight byte counters destroy sockets mid-stream if transfer limits are exceeded, preventing streaming memory bombs.","verify":"","src":"src/safe_proxy.js"},{"slug":"zip-slip-symlink-decompression-bomb-guard","q":"How to defend against Zip-Slip, symlink escapes, and 42.zip decompression bombs during untrusted archive ingestion?","problem":"Allowing users to upload ZIP archives for source code vulnerability auditing (OSV scanner) introduces severe vulnerabilities: directory traversal escaping the destination folder (Zip-Slip), symlink dereferencing reading host files, and 42.zip decompression bombs.","naive":[{"t":"Standard `unzip` Shell Executions","n":["Vulnerable to arbitrary file writes via crafted `../../` entry paths and creates dangling symlinks that point to `/etc/passwd` or `/proc/self/environ`."]},{"t":"Trusting ZIP Central Directory Headers","n":["Decompression bombs spoof the uncompressed size field in entry headers. Pre-extraction checks believe the file is 10 KB, while decompression yields gigabytes of memory-crashing zeroes."]},{"t":"Case-Insensitive File Clobbering","n":["Extracting archives containing both `file.js` and `FILE.js` on Windows or macOS APFS causes silent overwrites and security boundary bypasses."]}],"arch":"1. **POSIX Bitmask Inspection:** Inspects `(entry.externalFileAttributes >>> 16) & 0o170000` before opening entry streams. Symlinks (`0o120000`), FIFOs, and device nodes fail immediately.\n2. **4-Tier Quota Enforcement:** Validates entry count (max 20,000), total uncompressed bytes (max 500 MB), single-file ceiling (max 100 MB), and compression ratios (>200x for files >1 MB).\n3. **Stream-Level Chunk Interception:** An active `stream.on('data')` counter tracks written bytes in flight, aborting the stream and deleting file descriptors if headers lied.\n4. **Case-Folding & Exclusive Creation:** Tracks lowercase paths to stop case collisions, and writes files with `flags: 'wx', mode: 0o600` to ensure atomic, non-overwriting creation.","verify":"","src":"src/zip_security.js"},{"slug":"fail-closed-chromium-seccomp-container-boundary","q":"How to run untrusted headless browser workers under Docker without dropping sandbox isolation or using --no-sandbox?","problem":"Headless Chromium executing arbitrary third-party JavaScript is a high-value RCE target. In Docker environments, Chromium's namespace sandbox often fails under unprivileged users, tempting developers to run `--no-sandbox`, leaving the host container wide open to escape.","naive":[{"t":"Running with `--no-sandbox`","n":["Disables Chrome's multi-process sandbox completely. Any V8 JIT compiler exploit or Chromium memory corruption gives attackers direct code execution within the container."]},{"t":"Over-Privileged Containers (`--privileged` or `SYS_ADMIN`)","n":["Bypasses the error by granting the container administrative kernel privileges, allowing attackers to escape into the host kernel via cgroups or unconfined syscalls."]},{"t":"Sharing Environment Secret Variables","n":["Injecting backend environment variables into worker containers exposes database administrator credentials, billing keys, and OAuth secrets to untrusted renderers."]}],"arch":"1. **Fail-Closed Sandbox Enforcer:** Worker entrypoint explicitly rejects boot if `CHROME_NO_SANDBOX` is set to any truthy value.\n2. **Helper Binary Verification:** Validates that `/usr/lib/chromium/chrome-sandbox` is root-owned with the setuid bit active (`mode & 0o4000`), followed by a headless probe on `about:blank`.\n3. **Pinned Seccomp Profile:** Applies a SHA-256 verified Microsoft Playwright Seccomp profile with `SCMP_ACT_ERRNO` deny-by-default, dropping all capabilities (`cap_drop: ALL`), `read_only` root FS, and `no-new-privileges`.\n4. **Strict Secret Boundary:** `FORBIDDEN_WORKER_SECRETS` inspects environment variables on boot and immediately crashes if any auth, payment, or admin DB secrets are detected.","verify":"","src":"src/sandbox_enforcer.js"},{"slug":"webauthn-last-key-lock-and-hmac-recovery","q":"How to prevent accidental lockout from concurrent FIDO2/WebAuthn passkey deletions and secure break-glass recovery?","problem":"In high-security administrator planes mandating phishing-resistant FIDO2/WebAuthn hardware keys, concurrent deletion requests from two browser tabs can race past checks and delete the operator's last remaining passkey, locking them out permanently. Furthermore, plaintext recovery code storage creates credential leak hazards.","naive":[{"t":"Generic ORM Hook Validation","n":["Official authentication library plugin hooks often bypass generic application model hooks during passkey deletion, allowing concurrent deletes to succeed unnoticed."]},{"t":"Unserialized Client-Side Checks","n":["Checking passkey count in the browser before showing the delete button fails when an admin has the settings panel open in two separate browser tabs."]},{"t":"Plaintext Recovery Codes","n":["Storing backup codes in database columns allows database read leaks to expose permanent account takeover material."]}],"arch":"1. **Serialized Atomic Key Deletion:** Protects passkey deletion with application middleware and per-user atomic locking, re-evaluating credentials inside the lock and throwing `409 ADMIN_FINAL_PASSKEY_REQUIRED` if only one key remains.\n2. **Show-Once CSPRNG Recovery Codes:** 10 recovery codes generated with cryptographic randomness, shown only in the initial generation response, and stored strictly as HMAC-SHA256 hashes.\n3. **Session Revocation on Break-Glass:** Consuming a recovery code requires password + TOTP verification, consumes the single code, rotates out stale step-up markers, revokes active sessions, and increments the account's `security_version`.","verify":"","src":"src/passkey_lock.js"},{"slug":"streaming-aes-gcm-envelope-encryption-at-rest","q":"How to stream AES-256-GCM envelope encryption for multi-gigabyte source archives without V8 heap exhaustion?","problem":"Storing customer repository code archives on disk in plaintext creates severe regulatory and compliance violations. However, reading multi-hundred-megabyte ZIP files entirely into Node.js Buffer instances for encryption causes severe V8 garbage collection spikes and heap crashes under concurrent load.","naive":[{"t":"Plaintext Staging on Shared Mounts","n":["Vulnerable to unauthorized access from adjacent containers, compromised logs, or host backup exposures."]},{"t":"In-Memory Buffer Encryption (`fs.readFile` + `cipher.update`)","n":["Duplicates raw file payloads in V8 heap memory. Three concurrent 100 MB uploads allocate 600+ MB of RAM, triggering Out-Of-Memory container restarts."]},{"t":"Unauthenticated Cipher Modes (AES-CBC without HMAC)","n":["Vulnerable to padding oracle attacks and bit-flipping manipulation without cryptographic integrity verification."]}],"arch":"1. **WPA1 Binary Envelope Header:** Formats ciphertext with a compact 32-byte header: `WPA1` magic identifier (4 bytes), random CSPRNG IV (12 bytes), and GCM authentication tag (16 bytes).\n2. **Streaming Pipeline:** Pipes raw files through Node.js crypto ciphers directly to disk with `pipeline(fsReadStream, cipher, fsWriteStream, { mode: 0o600 })`.\n3. **Fixed-Size Memory Footprint:** Retains only the 32-byte envelope in memory while gigabyte payloads stream through chunk by chunk.\n4. **Isolated Ephemeral Decryption:** Workers decrypt into isolated staging directories on demand, run analysis, and immediately unlink plaintext files upon task completion.","verify":"","src":"src/envelope_encryption.js"},{"slug":"dynamic-spa-rendered-credential-leak-redaction","q":"How to sanitize credentials and session secrets leaked in dynamically rendered SPA DOM links and console diagnostics?","problem":"Headless browser analyzers crawling modern dynamic Single Page Applications (SPAs) frequently extract links rendered by client-side JavaScript. Many applications inadvertently expose authentication tokens, OAuth authorization codes, session keys, or API secrets in query parameters (`?token=xyz`, `?access_key=123`). Storing these links into shareable audit reports leaks sensitive customer secrets.","naive":[{"t":"Unfiltered Link Discovery","n":["Ingesting all `<a>` tags and dynamically rendered references exposes ephemeral tokens to database logs, report viewers, and external export APIs."]},{"t":"Raw Console Error Logging","n":["Browser `console.error` and unhandled rejection stack traces routinely dump authorization headers, cookies, and local file paths directly into diagnostic outputs."]},{"t":"Manual Blacklisting","n":["Relying on manual developer inspection fails against dynamic client-side router parameters generated at runtime."]}],"arch":"1. **In-Browser Credential Filter:** `RENDERED_LINK_SENSITIVE_PARAMETER` scans all query parameters for sensitive tokens (`token`, `secret`, `api_key`, `auth`, `session`), stripping offending parameters immediately.\n2. **Diagnostic Log Redaction:** `sanitizeDiagnosticText()` strips URLs down to clean origins and replaces sensitive headers (`authorization`, `cookie`, `set-cookie`, `token`, `password`) with `[REDACTED]`.\n3. **Stack Trace Scrubbing:** Masks local filesystem paths (`[LOCAL_PATH]`) and query strings from error stack traces before database persistence.","verify":"","src":"src/credential_redactor.js"},{"slug":"multi-network-bridge-segmentation-least-privilege","q":"How to prevent lateral movement from compromised worker containers using 5-tier Docker bridge segmentation?","problem":"Deploying microservices and background workers onto a single shared Docker bridge network allows compromised worker containers executing untrusted code to move laterally. An exploited browser worker can query internal database admin ports or harvest AI/payment credentials from adjacent microservices.","naive":[{"t":"Single Docker Bridge Network (`default`)","n":["Every container can reach every other container's open ports via internal DNS names, completely bypassing firewall isolation."]},{"t":"Container Host Networking (`network_mode: host`)","n":["Exposes host loopback interfaces, cloud instance metadata, and adjacent local daemons to containerized processes."]},{"t":"Bundling Credentials in API Workers","n":["Placing Stripe secret keys, Resend email keys, and AI provider credentials in a shared `.env` allows any container compromise to leak all business credentials."]}],"arch":"1. **5 Isolated Docker Bridge Networks:**\n   * `app-internal` (internal: true): Connects Frontend, API, and isolated internal workers.\n   * `data-internal` (internal: true): Connects API, Workers, and PostgreSQL without internet routing.\n   * `analysis-egress`: The *only* network with public internet access, attached exclusively to `analysis-worker`.\n   * `ai-egress`: Isolated egress attached strictly to `ai-service`.\n   * `email-egress`: Isolated egress attached strictly to `email-service`.\n2. **API Air-Gapping:** The core API backend has no analysis egress network attachment and cannot make unvetted outbound internet calls.\n3. **Microservice Token Isolation:** AI and email provider keys exist strictly inside `ai-service` and `email-service`; they are physically absent from the API and worker environments.","verify":"","src":"src/network_boundary.yml"},{"slug":"least-privilege-database-role-separation-at-startup","q":"How to enforce database least-privilege role separation and fail-closed worker startup attestation?","problem":"Most web backends connect to PostgreSQL using a single administrative or generic user (`postgres:postgres`). If a container running untrusted headless browser workloads is compromised via an RCE exploit, the attacker inherits full database privileges, allowing them to delete user accounts, drop audit tables, or dump payment secrets.","naive":[{"t":"Single Shared Database User","n":["Grants every microservice, background worker, and script identical full DML/DDL permissions across all tables."]},{"t":"Application-Layer Authorization Alone","n":["If an attacker gains shell execution inside a worker container, application-layer permission checks are bypassed; they talk directly to PostgreSQL."]},{"t":"Unvalidated Database Connections","n":["Containers misconfigured with wrong credentials silently connect with excessive privileges without alerting operators."]}],"arch":"1. **5 Segregated PostgreSQL Roles:**\n   * `wpa_owner` (NOLOGIN): Owns all platform tables.\n   * `wpa_migrator`: Exclusive DDL permissions for schema migrations.\n   * `wpa_runtime`: API backend user (SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE on application tables; no DDL privileges).\n   * `wpa_worker`: Browser analysis workers (SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE on scan/source tables; **no DELETE permissions** on event history, reports, or users).\n   * `wpa_maintenance`: Exclusive narrowly-scoped DELETE authority for data retention and confirmed workspace purging.\n2. **Fail-Closed Startup Role Attestation:** On boot, workers query `SELECT current_user AS role` and crash immediately with `WORKER_DATABASE_ROLE_MISMATCH` if the active role does not match `DATABASE_EXPECTED_ROLE`.\n3. **Mandatory Connection String Timeouts:** URL strings strictly enforce 4 timeouts: `connect_timeout`, `statement_timeout`, `lock_timeout`, and `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout`.","verify":"","src":"src/database_roles.sql"},{"slug":"subprocess-isolation-boundary-untrusted-native-scanners","q":"How to isolate untrusted native CLI security scanners and prevent host credential leakage?","problem":"Running native CLI security scanners (such as `osv-scanner`) against untrusted user code repositories introduces container breakout risks. Third-party scanner binaries can be tricked into reading local developer/server secrets (`~/.ssh/id_rsa`, `~/.gitconfig`, `.env`) or consuming unbounded RAM via inflated stdout JSON payloads.","naive":[{"t":"Direct Child Process Spawning (`child_process.exec`)","n":["Inherits all parent container environment variables (API secrets, database credentials) and runs under default shell execution vulnerable to command injection."]},{"t":"Standard Process Termination (`child.kill()`)","n":["On Linux/macOS, calling `kill()` on a parent process leaves orphaned child worker processes running in the background."]},{"t":"Persisting Customer Code in Memory","n":["Holding decrypted customer source archives in Node.js Buffers indefinitely exposes sensitive intellectual property to heap dumps and side-channel memory reads."]}],"arch":"1. **Isolated Subprocess Wrapper:** Enforces execution strictly through a dedicated `isolationRunner` sandbox wrapper. Direct execution is rejected fail-closed with `OSV_ISOLATION_UNAVAILABLE`.\n2. **Environment Stripping & Synthetic Home:** Wipes all environment variables except essential paths, explicitly setting `HOME: '/nonexistent'` to prevent scanners from reading developer dotfiles (`.ssh`, `.npmrc`).\n3. **Detached Process Tree Killing:** Spawns with `detached: true` on POSIX, allowing `process.kill(-child.pid, 'SIGKILL')` to terminate the entire process group cleanly upon timeout or client abort.\n4. **Stdout Byte Ceiling:** Stream-level listener tallies stdout chunks and kills the process immediately if output exceeds 25 MB (`maxOutputBytes`), neutralizing memory inflation attacks.\n5. **Zero-Fill RAM Buffer Overwrite:** Calls `context.sourceBuffer.fill(0)` immediately upon extraction, physically erasing proprietary source code from V8 memory.\n\n```javascript\n// 1. Spawning third-party CLI scanner within isolated sandbox policy\nif (!isolationRunner) throw new Error('OSV_ISOLATION_UNAVAILABLE');\n\nconst child = spawn(isolationRunner, [...isolationArgs, '--', executable, 'scan', 'source', '-r', root, '--format', 'json'], {\n    cwd: root, shell: false, windowsHide: true,\n    detached: process.platform !== 'win32',\n    env: { PATH: process.env.PATH || '', HOME: '/nonexistent', NO_COLOR: '1' },\n    stdio: ['ignore', 'pipe', 'pipe']\n});\n\n// 2. Kill entire process tree on abort/timeout and cap stdout inflation\nconst finish = (error, value) => {\n    clearTimeout(timer);\n    if (process.platform !== 'win32' && child.pid) process.kill(-child.pid, 'SIGKILL');\n    else child.kill('SIGKILL');\n};\n\n// 3. Cryptographic zero-fill RAM buffer wiping immediately after extraction\ncontext.sourceBuffer.fill(0);\n```","verify":"","src":"src/osv_sandbox.js"},{"slug":"fail-closed-maintenance-boundary-forbidden-secrets","q":"How to fail-closed destructive maintenance workers and verify forbidden secret absence on boot?","problem":"Background workers handling destructive operations—such as data retention pruning or GDPR workspace purging—typically run inside the same container image as API workers. If an API worker is compromised, possessing destructive deletion rights and billing/AI secrets in the same container creates an immense blast radius.","naive":[{"t":"Shared Container Environments","n":["Running maintenance tasks inside standard worker containers means destructive database credentials sit adjacent to Stripe secrets, AI keys, and Chromium binaries."]},{"t":"Assuming Correct Configuration","n":["Containers misconfigured by Compose or Kubernetes deployments silently run with excessive secrets without alerting operators."]},{"t":"Generic Database Roles","n":["Using standard database roles gives maintenance scripts authority to drop schema tables rather than strictly scoped row deletions."]}],"arch":"1. **Forbidden Secret Auditing on Boot:** Scans `process.env` against 9 prohibited credentials (`BETTER_AUTH_SECRET`, `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY`, `SOURCE_ENCRYPTION_KEY`). If even one secret is found, the container crashes with `MAINTENANCE_SECRET_BOUNDARY_VIOLATION`.\n2. **Mandatory Execution Disablement:** Requires `BROWSER_EXECUTION_DISABLED`, `SOURCE_EXECUTION_DISABLED`, and `PDF_EXECUTION_DISABLED` to all be true, preventing destructive workers from executing untrusted code.\n3. **Database Role Verification:** Queries `SELECT current_user AS role` and crashes if the active role does not match `wpa_maintenance` (which has scoped DELETE grants on retention tables and no DDL authority).\n4. **Enforced Connection Parameters:** Enforces `connect_timeout`, `statement_timeout`, `lock_timeout`, and mandatory TLS verification in production.\n\n```javascript\n// 1. Blacklist of forbidden credentials that must NEVER exist in maintenance\nconst FORBIDDEN_MAINTENANCE_SECRETS = Object.freeze([\n    'BETTER_AUTH_SECRET', 'GEMINI_API_KEY', 'STRIPE_SECRET_KEY',\n    'GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET', 'ADMIN_API_KEYS', 'SOURCE_ENCRYPTION_KEY',\n    'POSTGRES_ADMIN_PASSWORD', 'CHROME_PATH', 'QUEUE_DATABASE_URL'\n]);\n\n// 2. Boot-time fail-closed secret & engine boundary attestation\nfunction assertMaintenanceBoundary(env = process.env) {\n    if (env.EXECUTION_ROLE !== 'maintenance') throw new Error('MAINTENANCE_ROLE_REQUIRED');\n    \n    // Verify execution engines are strictly disabled\n    const disabled = ['BROWSER_EXECUTION_DISABLED', 'SOURCE_EXECUTION_DISABLED'].filter((k) => !truthy(env[k]));\n    if (disabled.length) throw new Error('MAINTENANCE_EXECUTION_DISABLED_REQUIRED: ' + disabled.join(', '));\n    \n    // Fail closed if any external API or admin secrets leaked in\n    const leaked = FORBIDDEN_MAINTENANCE_SECRETS.filter((k) => String(env[k] || '').length > 0);\n    if (leaked.length) throw new Error('MAINTENANCE_SECRET_BOUNDARY_VIOLATION: ' + leaked.join(', '));\n}\n```","verify":"","src":"src/maintenance_boundary.js"},{"slug":"webauthn-assertion-binding-and-monotonic-security-version","q":"How to prevent administrative step-up replay attacks using cryptographic WebAuthn assertion binding?","problem":"In administrative dashboards, step-up authentication tokens (proving a user recently touched a hardware passkey) are vulnerable to session replay attacks: if an attacker steals an elevated session cookie or token, they can replay privileged administrative operations across separate devices.","naive":[{"t":"Generic Step-Up Boolean Flags (`is_step_up=true`)","n":["Storing a simple timestamp or boolean flag in user session tables allows any active session for that user to inherit privileged status."]},{"t":"Token Replay Across IPs / Devices","n":["Compromised session tokens reused on different devices bypass traditional timeout checks if the step-up window remains active."]},{"t":"Flawed Privilege Revocation","n":["Invalidating elevated sessions upon password or permission changes typically requires sweeping Redis keys, which is error-prone and vulnerable to race conditions."]}],"arch":"1. **Cryptographic Assertion Binding:** When a WebAuthn hardware passkey is verified, the assertion is hashed alongside the client's IP, User-Agent, and session token:\n   `wpa_admin_webauthn_assertions(user_id, credential_id, ip_hash, user_agent_hash, session_id_hash)`.\n2. **Single-Use Partial Unique Index:** Enforces `UNIQUE (session_id_hash) WHERE session_id_hash IS NOT NULL`. An assertion can be bound strictly once; any attempt to replay it raises a unique constraint violation.\n3. **Atomic Consumption:** The assertion marker is marked `consumed_at = now()` in the same database transaction that elevates the session.\n4. **Monotonic Security Versioning:** Every administrator account carries a `security_version bigint`. Any password reset, 2FA rotation, or role update increments `security_version`. The middleware strictly compares:\n   `if (marker.securityVersion !== account.securityVersion) return false;`\n   Instantly invalidating all elevated sessions fleet-wide without scanning or deleting distributed session keys.\n\n```sql\n-- 1. Staged WebAuthn assertion bound cryptographically to session, IP, and User-Agent\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS wpa_admin_webauthn_assertions (\n    id text PRIMARY KEY,\n    user_id text NOT NULL REFERENCES \"user\"(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    credential_id text NOT NULL REFERENCES passkey(\"credentialID\") ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    ip_hash text NOT NULL,\n    user_agent_hash text NOT NULL,\n    session_id_hash text,\n    verified_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n    expires_at timestamptz NOT NULL,\n    consumed_at timestamptz,\n    CHECK (expires_at > verified_at)\n);\n\n-- 2. Partial unique index prevents assertion reuse across competing sessions\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS wpa_admin_webauthn_assertion_session_unique\n    ON wpa_admin_webauthn_assertions(session_id_hash)\n    WHERE session_id_hash IS NOT NULL;\n\n-- 3. Monotonic security_version increments invalidate all elevated step-up markers\nALTER TABLE wpa_admin_accounts ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS security_version bigint NOT NULL DEFAULT 1;\nALTER TABLE wpa_admin_reauth_markers ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS security_version bigint NOT NULL DEFAULT 1;\n```","verify":"","src":"src/webauthn_binding.sql"},{"slug":"llm-egress-secret-redaction-pipeline","q":"How to sanitize private RSA keys, database URIs, and cloud tokens before egressing audit traces to external LLMs?","problem":"When integrating third-party LLM providers (such as OpenAI, Gemini, or OpenRouter) to summarize audit findings or explain vulnerabilities, raw code snippets and error logs are transmitted across the network. If logs contain database connection strings, JWTs, Stripe keys, or private SSH/RSA keys, sending them unredacted exposes critical infrastructure to third-party data leaks, violating SOC 2, HIPAA, and GDPR compliance.","naive":[{"t":"Naive Keyword Filtering","n":["Replacing only known environment variable values fails when logs contain credentials generated at runtime or keys belonging to customers."]},{"t":"Unbounded Prompt Payloads","n":["Giant stack traces or code snippets can trigger token quota exhaustion or prompt injection attacks."]},{"t":"Null-Byte Injection Bypass","n":["Embedded null-bytes (`\\0`) can truncate strings in native C++ libraries or bypass naive regular expressions, leading to unscrubbed credential egress."]}],"arch":"1. **Null-Byte Scrubbing & Length Clamping:** `String(value).replace(/\\0/g, '').slice(0, maxLength)` neutralizes null-byte bypasses and bounds prompt memory before regex execution.\n2. **6 Frozen Pattern Filters:** Matches:\n   * Private keys (`-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----`) -> `[redacted-private-key]`\n   * JWT tokens (`eyJ...`) -> `[redacted-jwt]`\n   * Database connection strings (`postgres://user:pass@host`) -> `postgres://[redacted]@host`\n   * API tokens (Stripe `sk_live_`, OpenRouter `sk-or-v1-`, AWS `AKIA...`, Google `AIza...`) -> `[redacted-secret]`\n   * Environment variable exports (`export SECRET=...`) -> `[redacted-environment-variable]`\n3. **Structured Finding Normalization:** Normalizes titles, descriptions, and code snippets into an immutable object (`Object.freeze`), ensuring unredacted properties cannot be accidentally forwarded.\n\n```javascript\n// 1. Frozen cryptographic and credential token matching patterns\nconst SECRET_PATTERNS = Object.freeze([\n    [/-----BEGIN (?:RSA |EC |OPENSSH )?PRIVATE KEY-----[\\s\\S]*?-----END (?:RSA |EC |OPENSSH )?PRIVATE KEY-----/gi, '[redacted-private-key]'],\n    [/\\bBearer\\s+[A-Za-z0-9._~+/-]+=*/gi, 'Bearer [redacted]'],\n    [/\\bsk-or-v1-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{16,}\\b/gi, '[redacted-secret]'],\n    [/\\b(?:sk|rk|pk)_(?:live|test)_[A-Za-z0-9]{8,}\\b/gi, '[redacted-secret]'],\n    [/\\beyJ[A-Za-z0-9_-]{8,}\\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]{8,}\\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]{8,}\\b/g, '[redacted-jwt]'],\n    [/(\\b(?:https?|postgres(?:ql)?|mysql|mongodb(?:\\+srv)?|redis):\\/\\/)[^\\s/@:]+(?::[^\\s/@]*)?@/gi, '$1[redacted]@']\n]);\n\n// 2. Multi-stage sanitization pipeline stripping null-bytes and clamping length\nfunction redactText(value, { maxLength = 4_000 } = {}) {\n    let text = String(value ?? '').replace(/\\0/g, '').slice(0, maxLength);\n    for (const [pattern, replacement] of SECRET_PATTERNS) text = text.replace(pattern, replacement);\n    return text.trim();\n}\n```","verify":"Run the automated test suite using Node.js built-in test runner:\n\n```bash\nnode --test test/secret_redaction.test.js\n```\n\n### Verified Output:\n```text\n✔ redacts all 6 secret patterns reliably in arbitrary text (1.22ms)\n✔ pipeline strips null-bytes and clamps text to maxLength (0.17ms)\nℹ tests 2\nℹ suites 0\nℹ pass 2\nℹ fail 0\n```\n\n* **Test Environment:** Node.js v24.17.0, Windows 11, 2026-10-17 (no external dependencies, built-in node:test)*","src":"src/secret_redaction.js"},{"slug":"chromium-webrtc-udp-leak-quic-sandbox-flags","q":"How to prevent WebRTC STUN UDP leaks and HTTP/3 QUIC proxy bypasses in headless Chromium?","problem":"When routing automated headless Chromium crawlers through an egress security inspection proxy (`--proxy-server=http://proxy:8080`), Chromium by default can bypass the proxy across multiple network protocols:\n1. **WebRTC STUN/TURN UDP Leak:** WebRTC initiates peer-to-peer UDP connections directly to external STUN servers, completely bypassing HTTP/SOCKS proxies and broadcasting the host worker's true public and internal IP addresses.\n2. **HTTP/3 (QUIC) UDP Bypass:** Chromium defaults to HTTP/3 QUIC over UDP. Because most auditing proxies only inspect TCP, QUIC connections silently bypass inspection.\n3. **Loopback Bypass List Defaults:** By default, Chromium excludes localhost and loopback interfaces from proxying, allowing untrusted target pages to probe internal services (`http://127.0.0.1:9222`).","naive":[{"t":"Standard `--proxy-server` Flag Alone","n":["Configures proxying only for standard TCP HTTP/HTTPS traffic. WebRTC and QUIC UDP sockets are left completely unproxied."]},{"t":"Operating System Firewall Rules","n":["Restricting UDP at the container iptables layer causes WebRTC connection timeouts, freezing browser page load scripts that await STUN candidate discovery."]},{"t":"Default Proxy Bypass Defaults","n":["Chromium's internal default bypass list includes `localhost`, `127.0.0.1`, and `[::1]`, creating SSRF exposure to local debugging ports."]}],"arch":"1. **WebRTC IP Policy Enforcement:** Configures `--force-webrtc-ip-handling-policy=disable_non_proxied_udp`. Forces WebRTC to route strictly through the configured proxy or disable non-proxied UDP candidates entirely, eliminating host IP leaks.\n2. **QUIC Protocol Disablement:** Enforces `--disable-quic`, forcing all modern HTTP/3 connections to gracefully negotiate down to audited HTTP/2 and HTTP/1.1 TCP streams.\n3. **Mandatory Loopback Proxying:** Passes `--proxy-bypass-list=<-loopback>`. The `<-loopback>` syntax explicitly clears the default loopback bypass list, forcing even requests targeting `127.0.0.1` through the security proxy for validation and SSRF filtering.\n4. **Isolation Hardening:** Bundles `--dns-prefetch-disable`, `--disable-background-networking`, and `--disable-dev-shm-usage` to prevent covert channel communication.\n\n```javascript\n// Hardened Chromium sandbox launch flags enforcing all egress traffic through audit proxies\nfunction chromeFlags(proxyUrl, config = {}) {\n    const flags = [\n        '--headless=new',\n        '--disable-background-networking',\n        '--disable-default-apps',\n        '--disable-dev-shm-usage',\n        '--disable-extensions',\n        '--disable-quic', // Prevents UDP HTTP/3 from bypassing TCP inspection proxy\n        '--disable-sync',\n        '--dns-prefetch-disable',\n        '--force-webrtc-ip-handling-policy=disable_non_proxied_udp', // Blocks WebRTC host IP leaks\n        '--metrics-recording-only',\n        '--no-default-browser-check',\n        '--no-first-run',\n        `--proxy-server=${proxyUrl}`,\n        '--proxy-bypass-list=<-loopback>' // Prevents bypass for loopback and LAN endpoints\n    ];\n    if (config.chromeNoSandbox) flags.push('--no-sandbox', '--disable-setuid-sandbox');\n    return flags;\n}\n```","verify":"","src":"src/browser_sandbox_flags.js"},{"slug":"web-crypto-hs256-jwt-key-hardening","q":"How to build a zero-dependency HS256 JWT engine on Web Crypto with 256-bit key length enforcement and constant-time verification?","problem":"1. **Weak HMAC Symmetric Keys:** Naive JWT implementations accept arbitrary 4-character passwords as secrets, allowing offline dictionary brute-forcing via tools like hashcat.\n2. **Algorithm Confusion Attacks:** Accepting dynamic `alg` headers allows attackers to forge tokens with `alg: \"none\"` or submit RSA public keys against HMAC verification logic.\n3. **Timing Attacks on Signatures:** Comparing signatures with naive string comparison (`sig1 === sig2`) leaks byte equality offsets via microsecond timing side-channels.","naive":[{"t":"Heavy Third-Party Dependencies","n":["Pulling full cryptographic packages adds hundreds of kilobytes and Node.js-only bindings that break on Cloudflare Workers and browser runtimes."]},{"t":"Lenient Secret Coercion","n":["Silently padding short keys weakens entropy and violates RFC 7519 cryptographic recommendations."]},{"t":"Unvalidated JSON Decoding","n":["Decoding payloads without fatal UTF-8 rejection allows Unicode surrogate pair attacks and prototype pollution."]}],"arch":"1. **256-Bit Key Enforcement (`MIN_SECRET_BYTES = 32`):** `encodeSecret()` strictly validates that the shared secret is at least 32 UTF-8 bytes (256 bits), throwing a fail-closed `RangeError` on weak keys.\n2. **Universal Web Crypto API:** Built entirely on `globalThis.crypto.subtle`, running natively with zero external dependencies in Cloudflare Workers, Node.js, Deno, Bun, and browsers.\n3. **Constant-Time Verification:** Signatures are verified natively via `crypto.subtle.verify('HMAC', ...)`, eliminating timing attacks.","verify":"","src":"src/jwt_validator.ts"},{"slug":"fail-closed-static-ssr-rce-boundary","q":"How to fail-closed static SSR boundaries to eliminate server-side remote code execution in untrusted component markup?","problem":"Server-Side Rendering (SSR) in component frameworks typically evaluates component JavaScript on the backend Node.js thread. If untrusted users or CMS contributors submit component markup containing expressions (e.g. `{process.exit()}` or `{require('child_process').exec(...)}`), evaluating this template on the server grants attackers full Remote Code Execution (RCE) on backend infrastructure.","naive":[{"t":"Arbitrary Code Evaluation in SSR","n":["Running `eval()` or executing compiled JS in SSR environments executes untrusted code with full server process permissions."]},{"t":"Partial Render Leakage","n":["Silently dropping dynamic directives in SSR produces broken HTML that causes jarring visual shifts when client hydration executes."]},{"t":"XSS via Unescaped Attributes","n":["Concatenating attribute strings without escaping double quotes and single quotes enables attribute breakout XSS attacks (`<div title=\"${input}\">`)."]}],"arch":"1. **AST AST Rejection of Dynamic Directives:** `renderStaticNode()` inspects template node types. If it encounters `Expression`, `IfBlock`, `EachBlock`, or dynamic `bindings`/`events`, it immediately throws without evaluating code.\n2. **Fail-Closed Fallback Mount Point:** The SSR helper catches dynamic components and returns a safe client-rendered placeholder: `<div id=\"app\" data-flow-client-render></div>`.\n3. **Rigorous HTML & Attribute Escaping:**\n   * `escapeHtml()`: Sanitizes `&`, `<`, `>`.\n   * `escapeAttribute()`: Replaces `\"` with `&quot;` and `'` with `&#39;`, blocking attribute breakout injection.\n4. **No Server-Side Code Execution:** The server stringifier never invokes `new Function()` or `eval()`; templates are never evaluated on the server and operate purely on static string AST representations.\n\n```typescript\n// 1. Strict AST validation rejecting dynamic expressions and event handlers\nfunction renderStaticNode(node: TemplateNode): string {\n  if (node.type === 'Text') return escapeHtml(node.content);\n  if (node.type === 'Expression' || node.type === 'IfBlock' || node.type === 'EachBlock') {\n    throw new Error(`Static SSR cannot evaluate ${node.type}; render this component on the client`);\n  }\n  if (Object.keys(node.bindings).length > 0 || Object.keys(node.events).length > 0) {\n    throw new Error('Static SSR cannot preserve bindings or events; render this component on the client');\n  }\n\n  const attributes = Object.entries(node.attributes)\n    .map(([key, value]) => ` ${key}=\"${escapeAttribute(value)}\"`)\n    .join('');\n  if (VOID_ELEMENTS.has(node.tag)) return `<${node.tag}${attributes}>`;\n  return `<${node.tag}${attributes}>${node.children.map(renderStaticNode).join('')}</${node.tag}>`;\n}\n\n// 2. Safe client mount point fallback preventing server process crashes\nexport function renderToString(source: string): StaticRenderResult {\n  const component = parse(source);\n  const analysis = analyze(component);\n  if (analysis.strategy !== 'SSG') {\n    return { html: '<div id=\"app\" data-flow-client-render></div>', rendered: false };\n  }\n  return { html: renderStaticComponent(component), rendered: true };\n}\n```","verify":"","src":"src/static_ssr_boundary.ts"}],"dir":"02-security-and-sandboxing","blurb":"Mid-flight pause, SSRF, seccomp, WebAuthn, envelope encryption"},{"n":3,"name":"Concurrency & Race Conditions","cases":[{"slug":"hardware-collision-avoidance-yield","q":"How to eliminate hardware mouse and keyboard race conditions between humans and autonomous web agents?","problem":"In co-pilot browser automation, an AI agent and a human user share access to the same active browser window. \n\nIf the agent dispatches synthetic CDP mouse events (`Input.dispatchMouseEvent`) while the user simultaneously moves their physical mouse, coordinate race conditions occur: the agent clicks shifted coordinates, form inputs lose focus, and unintended buttons are triggered.","naive":[{"t":"Locking User Peripherals","n":["Blocking OS-level input while the AI navigates destroys ergonomics and violates basic user agency."]},{"t":"Modal Overlays","n":["Dimming the screen with \"AI is running, please wait\" prevents the human from interacting when the AI gets stuck."]}],"arch":"We engineered a passive Hardware Yield Protocol:\n\n1. **Passive Event Traps:** We register `{ passive: true }` listeners for `mousemove`, `mousedown`, `keydown`, and `wheel`. Passive listeners never block the main thread.\n2. **Intervention State:** When physical input is detected, `window.__humanIntervening` is asserted immediately.\n3. **Adaptive Debounce:** An 1800ms debounce window prevents flickering and yields control to the human.\n4. **Autonomous Yield Loop:** The agent execution engine awaits `waitForHumanYield()` prior to any CDP dispatch, cleanly yielding CPU time slices until the user releases the controls.","verify":"","src":"src/collision_avoidance.ts"},{"slug":"postgres-advisory-lock-last-super-admin-race","q":"How to eliminate the concurrent dual-downgrade race condition on privileged accounts under PostgreSQL READ COMMITTED?","problem":"A critical system invariant requires that at least one active `super_admin` must exist at all times. Under PostgreSQL's default `READ COMMITTED` transaction isolation, row-level check triggers fail to prevent concurrent downgrade attacks: if two admins downgrade each other simultaneously, both transactions see 2 admins in their snapshot and commit, leaving 0 super admins.","naive":[{"t":"Application-Layer Count Checks","n":["`SELECT count(*) FROM admins` executed in Node.js before an update suffers from a classic Time-of-Check to Time-of-Use race condition across concurrent requests."]},{"t":"Row-Level Triggers Alone","n":["Under `READ COMMITTED`, row triggers evaluate each row independently. Transaction A locks Admin 1; Transaction B locks Admin 2. Both see the other admin still active in their snapshot and both succeed."]},{"t":"Table-Level Exclusive Locks (`LOCK TABLE`)","n":["Too coarse; blocks non-conflicting reads and creates database-wide lock contention during high-frequency admin telemetry updates."]}],"arch":"1. **Statement-Level Transaction Advisory Lock:** Migration 041 installs a statement-level trigger acquiring `pg_advisory_xact_lock(hashtext('wpa:privileged-role-management'))` BEFORE any UPDATE or DELETE statement executes.\n2. **Deterministic Serialization:** Concurrent mutations on the admin set are strictly serialized before row evaluation begins.\n3. **Fail-Closed Row Guard:** Migration 040's row trigger evaluates the active super admin count. The first transaction succeeds; the competing transaction evaluates the newly committed state, detects 0 remaining super admins, and raises SQLSTATE `23514` (`LAST_SUPER_ADMIN_REQUIRED`).\n4. **Automatic Lock Release:** Advisory transaction locks release automatically upon transaction commit or rollback, leaving no orphaned locks.","verify":"","src":"src/super_admin_guard.sql"},{"slug":"row-advisory-lock-workspace-seat-limits","q":"How to strictly enforce multi-tenant seat allocation limits under concurrent invitation spikes using PostgreSQL triggers?","problem":"In SaaS applications with plan-based member seat limits (e.g., Studio plan = 5 seats), concurrent user invitations cause race conditions. If two admins approve invitations at the same millisecond when 4 seats are filled, both queries read 4 occupied seats and commit, resulting in 6 active members on a 5-seat plan.","naive":[{"t":"Application-Layer ORM Validation","n":["Checking seat counts in Node.js before inserting a membership record fails completely under concurrent API calls due to network latency and asynchronous event loops."]},{"t":"Global Database Locks","n":["Locking the entire `member` table halts membership invitations for all tenants across the entire platform."]},{"t":"Database Check Constraints","n":["Standard SQL `CHECK` constraints cannot execute dynamic subqueries across multiple tables (`wpa_plan_catalog`, `wpa_entitlement_grants`, and `member`)."]}],"arch":"1. **Per-Organization Advisory Lock:** The trigger executes `PERFORM pg_advisory_xact_lock(hashtext('workspace-seat:' || NEW.\"organizationId\"))`. Only concurrent operations on the *same* workspace serialize; other workspaces proceed in parallel.\n2. **Dynamic Effective Plan Resolution:** Queries active temporary promotional upgrades (`wpa_entitlement_grants`) before falling back to the base workspace plan.\n3. **Atomic Occupancy Check:** Accurately counts occupied members against catalog limits and throws `WORKSPACE_SEAT_LIMIT_REACHED` before any row is committed.\n4. **Transaction Scoping:** Advisory locks automatically release upon statement commit or rollback without manual cleanup.","verify":"","src":"src/workspace_seat_limit.sql"},{"slug":"postgres-distributed-mutex-scanner-serialization","q":"How to build a distributed FIFO mutex for heavy vulnerability scanning workers without Redis or external queue bloat?","problem":"Dynamic security scanners like OWASP ZAP are stateful and CPU/RAM heavy. When multiple worker containers process crawl queues concurrently, bombarding a single ZAP daemon causes session cross-pollution, crashed daemon processes, and corrupted scan reports.","naive":[{"t":"Spinning Up External Redis Mutexes (Redlock)","n":["Adds operational complexity, network failure modes, separate cluster health checks, and memory management for a single serialized task."]},{"t":"Application Process Mutexes","n":["In-memory Node.js mutexes only serialize threads within a single container; they cannot coordinate across multiple containerized worker instances."]},{"t":"Queue Concurrency = 1","n":["Restricting entire worker queues to concurrency: 1 starves other lightweight non-ZAP tasks (Lighthouse, Axe, broken link checks)."]}],"arch":"1. **Postgres Session Advisory Lock:** Uses `SELECT pg_try_advisory_lock(hashtext('wpa-zap-baseline'))` on a dedicated database pool client.\n2. **Dedicated Connection Retain:** The acquired connection is held open for the exact duration of the scan, tying the advisory lock lifetime directly to the TCP socket.\n3. **AbortSignal & Timeout Integration:** Propagates client cancellations and job timeouts via `signal.throwIfAborted()`, ensuring clean socket release without hung locks.\n4. **Idempotent Cleanup Hook:** Returns an asynchronous release function that unlocks the advisory mutex and releases the client back to the pool.","verify":"","src":"src/zap_lock.js"},{"slug":"postgres-atomic-sliding-window-rate-limit","q":"How to implement atomic sliding window rate limiting in PostgreSQL without Redis or Lua scripts?","problem":"Implementing sliding-window rate limiting across distributed containerized workers typically leads teams to deploy Redis clusters and maintain custom Lua scripts. Adding Redis adds operational complexity, memory eviction issues, and network failure modes.","naive":[{"t":"In-Memory Rate Limiters (Node.js Map/LRU)","n":["Only protects a single process. In multi-container topologies behind load balancers, memory counters are fragmented, allowing N-times traffic multiplication."]},{"t":"Standard Redis + Lua Scripts","n":["Requires managing separate Redis clusters, Sentinel/failover configuration, and eviction policies, introducing a second stateful infrastructure dependency."]},{"t":"Multi-Query Read-Then-Write SQL","n":["Running `SELECT hits` followed by `UPDATE hits` introduces severe race conditions under concurrent burst traffic, allowing limits to be exceeded."]}],"arch":"1. **Atomic Upsert Invariant:** Executes an atomic `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (namespace, key) DO UPDATE` in a single database roundtrip.\n2. **In-Database Window Evaluation:** Evaluates whether `window_started_at` has exceeded the sliding duration directly inside SQL `CASE` expressions:\n   * If the window expired: Resets `window_started_at` to `now()` and sets `hits` to 1.\n   * If the window is active: Retains `window_started_at` and increments `hits + 1`.\n3. **Immediate Atomic Return:** The query returns `RETURNING hits, window_started_at`, providing exact count and calculated reset time without advisory locks.","verify":"","src":"src/rate_limiter.sql"},{"slug":"distributed-job-lease-heartbeat-cas-settlement","q":"How to fence zombie worker overwrites during long-running distributed analysis tasks using heartbeat leases and CAS?","problem":"In distributed architectures processing long-running browser analyses (multi-page audits taking minutes), worker nodes can experience temporary network partitions or high CPU freezes. When a queue manager assumes the job failed and reassigns it, the original worker can revive and overwrite the new worker's results with stale data (the \"Zombie Worker\" problem).","naive":[{"t":"Static Job Timeouts","n":["Setting a fixed timeout (e.g., 60 seconds) causes complex 50-page audits to be prematurely reassigned while still actively and legitimately computing."]},{"t":"Unfenced Database Writes","n":["Writing results with `UPDATE jobs SET status = 'completed'` allows whichever worker finishes last to overwrite database state, regardless of whether its claim was expired."]},{"t":"Unbounded Retention Deletion","n":["Automated cleanup workers cleaning up old artifacts can be tricked via path traversal into unlinking system or parent directories."]}],"arch":"1. **Heartbeat Lease Renewal:** An unref'd timer executes every `leaseMs / 3`, extending `lease_expires_at` in PostgreSQL as long as the worker process remains healthy.\n2. **Compare-And-Swap (CAS) Settlement:** When settling job completion, the worker passes its unique `leaseOwner` and `leaseToken`. If a timeout expired the lease and reassigned the job, the CAS query updates 0 rows, gracefully rejecting the zombie write.\n3. **Contained Path Sandboxing:** File cleanup routines use `isContainedPath(root, target)` to guarantee that retention workers can never delete files outside explicit artifact directories.","verify":"","src":"src/lease_heartbeat.js"},{"slug":"canonicalized-json-idempotency-fingerprint","q":"How to prevent state corruption from mutated payloads under reused Idempotency-Keys using canonical JSON SHA-256 fingerprinting?","problem":"Standard HTTP API idempotency implementations rely solely on the `Idempotency-Key` HTTP header. If a malicious or buggy client sends a modified request body using an existing idempotency key, the server may either blindly return the cached response of the earlier request (serving mismatched data) or re-execute the mutation under the conflicting key. Furthermore, JSON serialization has non-deterministic object key order (`{\"a\":1,\"b\":2}` vs `{\"b\":2,\"a\":1}`), causing naive hashing to trigger false mismatch errors.","naive":[{"t":"Header-Only Idempotency Validation","n":["Checking only `key = store.get(header)` fails to detect payload tampering or client parameter divergence."]},{"t":"Raw JSON String Hashing","n":["Computing `sha256(JSON.stringify(req.body))` fails when clients or proxies alter key order or formatting, producing false 409 conflict errors for semantically identical requests."]},{"t":"Database-Level Race Windows","n":["Committing mutations before verifying payload hashes risks partial state corruption if parallel requests race into the endpoint."]}],"arch":"1. **Recursive Canonicalization:** `canonicalize()` traverses arrays and objects recursively, applying `Object.keys(value).sort()` at every nesting level to ensure deterministic JSON representation regardless of serialization order.\n2. **Cryptographic SHA-256 Fingerprinting:** Computes `sha256(JSON.stringify(canonicalize(body)))`, generating a stable 64-character hexadecimal digest.\n3. **Fail-Closed Replay Assertion:** `assertIdempotentReplay()` cross-references the requested fingerprint with the existing stored transaction. If fingerprints diverge, it immediately throws HTTP 409 `IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REUSED`.\n4. **Header Normalization:** Enforces strict regex validation (`^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._:-]{7,199}$`) on idempotency keys to reject empty, malformed, or excessively long headers.\n\n```javascript\n// 1. Recursive deterministic JSON object key sorting\nfunction canonicalize(value) {\n    if (value === null || typeof value !== 'object') return value;\n    if (Array.isArray(value)) return value.map(canonicalize);\n    return Object.fromEntries(Object.keys(value).sort().map((key) => [key, canonicalize(value[key])]));\n}\n\n// 2. Cryptographic payload fingerprint calculation\nfunction operationFingerprint(value) {\n    return crypto.createHash('sha256').update(JSON.stringify(canonicalize(value))).digest('hex');\n}\n\n// 3. Fail-closed replay verification preventing key reuse for mutated payloads\nfunction assertIdempotentReplay(existingFingerprint, requestedFingerprint) {\n    if (existingFingerprint && requestedFingerprint && existingFingerprint !== requestedFingerprint) {\n        throw new AppError('The idempotency key was already used for a different request.', {\n            status: 409,\n            code: 'IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REUSED'\n        });\n    }\n}\n```","verify":"Run the automated test suite using Node.js built-in test runner:\n\n```bash\nnode --test test/idempotency.test.js\n```\n\n### Verified Output:\n```text\n✔ canonicalize sorts object keys recursively regardless of insertion order (1.08ms)\n✔ mutated payload produces different fingerprint and triggers 409 replay rejection (0.59ms)\nℹ tests 2\nℹ suites 0\nℹ pass 2\nℹ fail 0\n```\n\n* **Test Environment:** Node.js v24.17.0, Windows 11, 2026-10-17 (no external dependencies, built-in node:test and node:crypto)*","src":"src/idempotency.js"},{"slug":"unhandled-queue-event-fail-closed-provisioning","q":"How to prevent Node.js process crashes during planned PostgreSQL restarts and eliminate queue migration deadlocks?","problem":"In PostgreSQL-backed job queues (such as `pg-boss`), background idle connection drops or planned PostgreSQL maintenance restarts emit errors directly onto the `PgBoss` EventEmitter. In Node.js, an EventEmitter with an unhandled `error` event immediately triggers an `uncaughtException`, crashing the entire container. Furthermore, allowing workers or API processes to execute opportunistic DDL (`migrate: true`) causes schema migration deadlocks during multi-container rolling deployments.","naive":[{"t":"Missing EventEmitter Error Handler","n":["By default, `boss.on('error')` is not registered. When the database pool closes during a failover or restart, Node.js terminates with code 1."]},{"t":"Opportunistic DDL on Application Boot","n":["If API containers and worker containers both boot with auto-migration enabled, they race to create tables and execute `CREATE INDEX`, causing transaction deadlocks."]},{"t":"Silent Dispatch to Typo Queues","n":["Sending jobs to unprovisioned queues without validation causes jobs to sit silently in database tables without active workers."]}],"arch":"1. **Explicit Error Boundary:** Binds a top-level `this.boss.on('error', ...)` listener. Logs the event cleanly with error codes and absorbs transient network drops so the container remains alive during planned DB maintenance.\n2. **No-Migration Runtime Invariant (`migrate: false`):** Disables auto-migration in application code. Queue schema creation is strictly isolated to dedicated deployment CI/CD migration scripts.\n3. **Canonical Queue Validation (`ensureQueue`):**\n   * Validates queue names against frozen `QUEUE_DEFINITIONS`. Unknown queues throw immediately.\n   * Calls `await this.boss.getQueue(name)`. If a queue was not provisioned during release migration, it fails closed rather than failing silently.\n\n```javascript\n// 1. Explicit EventEmitter error listener preventing uncaughtException on DB restart\nthis.boss = new BossClass({ ...connection, schema: 'wpa_queue', migrate: false });\nthis.boss.on?.('error', (error) => {\n    try {\n        queueLogger?.error?.('PostgreSQL queue connection failed', {\n            component: 'platform-queue',\n            errorCode: error?.code || 'POSTGRES_QUEUE_ERROR',\n            error\n        });\n    } catch { /* Logger failure must never terminate API process */ }\n});\n\n// 2. Canonical queue enforcement failing closed on unprovisioned queues\nasync ensureQueue(name) {\n    if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(QUEUE_DEFINITIONS, name)) {\n        throw new Error(`Queue ${name} is not a canonical worker queue.`);\n    }\n    await this.start();\n    if (this.queues.has(name)) return;\n    const queue = await this.boss.getQueue(name);\n    if (!queue) throw new Error(`Queue ${name} is not provisioned.`);\n    this.queues.add(name);\n}\n```","verify":"","src":"src/queue_boundary.js"},{"slug":"hierarchical-composite-rate-limit-partitioning","q":"How to eliminate corporate NAT rate-limit starvation and implement atomic sliding windows in PostgreSQL?","problem":"Standard API rate limiters rely either on raw IP addresses or simple API key strings.\n1. **IP-Only Limits Break Corporate NATs:** Large enterprise organizations share single egress IP addresses across thousands of employees. A single automated script can exhaust the rate limit for the entire corporate office.\n2. **API-Key-Only Limits Cause Internal Starvation:** In multi-user workspaces, a misconfigured script using one API key can starve other workspace members.\n3. **Redis Dependency Bloat:** Standard sliding-window rate limiters require running Redis clusters with custom Lua scripts, adding infrastructure complexity.","naive":[{"t":"Noisy Neighbor Starvation","n":["Global IP keys cause multi-tenant workspaces behind shared corporate proxies to constantly encounter HTTP 429 errors."]},{"t":"Redis Failover Outages","n":["When Redis goes down, APIs either fail open (allowing DDoS attacks) or fail closed (taking down the entire platform)."]},{"t":"Application Memory Leaks","n":["In-memory `Map` stores do not synchronize across multi-pod container deployments, allowing clients to bypass limits by cycling across pods."]}],"arch":"1. **Hierarchical Composite Key Resolution:**\n   * If a workspace identity exists: resolves to `workspace:{id}:key:{apiKeyId}` (if authenticated via key) or `workspace:{id}:ip:{ip}` (if authenticated via web session).\n   * Unauthenticated traffic falls back cleanly to normalized IP keys (`ip:{ip}`).\n2. **Selective Route Skipping:** `isScanProgressRead()` automatically bypasses rate limiting for high-frequency SSE progress polling endpoints (`/scans/:id/events`).\n3. **Atomic Single-Query SQL Sliding Window:** Uses PostgreSQL `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (namespace, key) DO UPDATE` with inline `CASE` evaluation. In 1 network roundtrip, it resets the window if expired, increments hits if active, and returns current hit counts atomically.\n\n```javascript\n// 1. Hierarchical composite key generator preventing corporate NAT starvation\nfunction clientKey(request) {\n    if (request.platformIdentity?.workspaceId) {\n        return `workspace:${request.platformIdentity.workspaceId}:${request.apiKeyId ? `key:${request.apiKeyId}` : `ip:${ipKeyGenerator(request.ip)}`}`;\n    }\n    if (request.apiKeyId) return `key:${request.apiKeyId}`;\n    return `ip:${ipKeyGenerator(request.ip)}`;\n}\n\n// 2. Atomic single-query PostgreSQL sliding window state machine without Redis\nasync increment(key, windowMs = 900_000) {\n    const { rows } = await this.pool.query(\n        `INSERT INTO wpa_rate_limit_buckets(namespace,key,window_started_at,hits)\n         VALUES($1,$2,now(),1)\n         ON CONFLICT(namespace,key) DO UPDATE SET\n            window_started_at=CASE WHEN wpa_rate_limit_buckets.window_started_at <= now()-($3::text || ' milliseconds')::interval THEN now() ELSE wpa_rate_limit_buckets.window_started_at END,\n            hits=CASE WHEN wpa_rate_limit_buckets.window_started_at <= now()-($3::text || ' milliseconds')::interval THEN 1 ELSE wpa_rate_limit_buckets.hits+1 END,\n            updated_at=now()\n         RETURNING hits,window_started_at`, [this.namespace, key, windowMs]);\n    return { totalHits: Number(rows[0].hits) };\n}\n```","verify":"","src":"src/composite_rate_limiter.js"},{"slug":"nonblocking-try-lock-audio-buffer-swap","q":"How to extract 1024-point audio buffers for real-time FFT spectrum visualizers without audio thread mutex blocking?","problem":"1. **Audio Driver Underruns (Buffer Starvation):** The real-time OS audio thread (WASAPI/CoreAudio/ALSA) has strict hard real-time deadlines (~5-10ms). If the audio thread acquires a standard blocking mutex while the UI thread is executing a heavy Fast Fourier Transform (FFT), audio playback stutters and glitching occurs.\n2. **Memory Allocation on Audio Thread:** Allocating new vectors or cloning 1024-float arrays on every audio frame triggers system memory allocators, introducing unpredictable latency spikes.\n3. **Desynchronized Visualizations:** Real-time frequency bars must display recent audio without lag while never impacting playback performance.","naive":[{"t":"Standard `mutex.lock()` on Audio Thread","n":["The cardinal rule of audio DSP: NEVER wait on a lock in the audio render callback. Priority inversion causes instantaneous audio dropouts."]},{"t":"Channel-Based MPSC Queues","n":["Queuing unconsumed buffers fills memory if the UI visualizer slows down or is minimized."]},{"t":"Cross-Thread Vector Cloning","n":["Cloning 1024-element buffers thousands of times per second wastes memory bandwidth."]}],"arch":"1. **Non-Blocking Mutex Acquisition (`try_lock()`):** The audio thread attempts acquisition via `self.analyzer.try_lock()`. If the visualizer thread holds the lock, the audio thread drops the frame without waiting and continues rendering audio uninterrupted.\n2. **Zero-Copy Vector Swap (`std::mem::swap`):** Swapping buffer pointers via `std::mem::swap(&mut *target, &mut self.analysis_buffer)` transfers ownership in constant O(1) time without heap allocations.\n3. **Decoupled Atomic Flag (`AtomicBool`):** An atomic flag (`analysis_enabled`) checks whether visualizers are visible; if hidden, audio sample accumulation is disabled without locking.","verify":"","src":"src/fft_buffer_swap.rs"},{"slug":"nested-reactive-batch-deduplication","q":"How to eliminate cascading DOM reflows and premature flushes in nested reactive updates using depth tracking and Set deduplication?","problem":"When multiple reactive signals mutate in sequence (e.g. updating `setX(1)`, `setY(2)`, `setZ(3)` inside a click handler or network callback), a naive reactive engine immediately notifies subscribers on each write. This causes 3 separate DOM updates and cascading effect executions. Furthermore, if batches are nested (`batch(() => { batch(...) })`), naive implementations flush prematurely on the inner batch, triggering visual glitches and race conditions.","naive":[{"t":"Immediate Synchronous Dispatch","n":["Updating multiple related signals causes effects to observe intermediate, inconsistent states (Glitching)."]},{"t":"Premature Inner Batch Flushing","n":["Resetting batch state when an inner function exits executes effects before the outer transaction finishes mutating state."]},{"t":"Duplicate Effect Invocations","n":["An effect depending on both Signal A and Signal B would run twice without a deduplicated queue."]}],"arch":"1. **Object.is Value Short-Circuiting:** Compares new values against existing values via `Object.is(value, newValue)`. Writing identical values (including `NaN === NaN`) returns immediately without queuing effects.\n2. **Re-entrant Depth Tracking:** `batchDepth++` increments upon entering and decrements in a mandatory `finally` block.\n3. **Atomic Outermost Flush:** Queued effects are collected in `batchedEffects = new Set<() => void>()`. Effects only flush when `batchDepth === 0`, ensuring nested batches coalesce cleanly into a single execution tick.\n4. **Snapshot Execution Queue:** Copies queued callbacks into `new Set(batchedEffects)` before clearing, preventing infinite queues if effects mutate further state during flush.\n\n```typescript\n// 1. Short-circuit identical writes using Object.is equality\nconst set = (newValue: T) => {\n  if (!Object.is(value, newValue)) {\n    value = newValue;\n    if (batchDepth > 0) {\n      subscribers.forEach(sub => batchedEffects.add(sub));\n    } else {\n      const effectsToRun = new Set(subscribers);\n      effectsToRun.forEach(effect => effect());\n    }\n  }\n};\n\n// 2. Atomic nested batching with global depth counter\nexport function batch(fn: () => void) {\n  batchDepth++;\n  try {\n    fn();\n  } finally {\n    batchDepth--;\n    if (batchDepth === 0) {\n      const effectsToRun = new Set(batchedEffects);\n      batchedEffects.clear();\n      effectsToRun.forEach(effect => effect());\n    }\n  }\n}\n```","verify":"Run the automated test suite using Node.js built-in test runner with native TypeScript type stripping (requires Node 22.6+):\n\n```bash\nnode --experimental-strip-types --test test/nested_batching.test.ts\n```\n\n### Verified Output:\n```text\n✔ nested batching coalesces multiple writes into a single effect execution (0.84ms)\n✔ Object.is short-circuits identical writes without triggering effects (0.19ms)\nℹ tests 2\nℹ suites 0\nℹ pass 2\nℹ fail 0\n```\n\n* **Test Environment:** Node.js v24.17.0, Windows 11, 2026-10-17 (no external dependencies, native type stripping)*","src":"src/nested_batching.ts"}],"dir":"03-concurrency-and-race-conditions","blurb":"Advisory locks, distributed mutexes, sliding window upserts"},{"n":4,"name":"Memory & Performance","cases":[{"slug":"ram-level-network-reverse-scraping","q":"How to reverse-scrape structured API responses directly from browser RAM?","problem":"Standard autonomous web agents parse the HTML DOM tree to extract information for LLM reasoning loops. \n\nOn modern Single Page Applications (SPAs), dumping the DOM yields thousands of tokens of wrapper elements, CSS class names, and SVG markup. This inflates context windows with redundant markup and increases reasoning latency.","naive":[{"t":"HTML Sanitization (readability.js, cheerio)","n":["Drops crucial dynamic client-side hydration data and still produces unstructured tokens."]},{"t":"Vision Screenshots","n":["High token cost, coordinate inaccuracy, and fails on off-screen lazy-loaded lists."]}],"arch":"Instead of parsing what the browser rendered, we intercept the authoritative API responses directly from Chrome's RAM:\n\n1. **Network Sniffing:** We subscribe to CDP `Network.responseReceived`.\n2. **Filter & In-Memory Trap:** Incoming responses matching `application/json` or API patterns have their `requestId` registered.\n3. **RAM Reverse-Scraping:** We issue `Network.getResponseBody`. Chrome returns the raw JSON payload directly from its internal cache without prompt token overhead.","verify":"","src":"src/reverse_scraping.ts"},{"slug":"in-browser-aabb-collision-and-wcag-luminance-math","q":"How to audit responsive UI collisions and WCAG contrast without vision models or screenshots?","problem":"Auditing responsive web pages for visual interface bugs—such as overlapping modals, obscured buttons, or unreadable low-contrast text—is typically done with screenshot OCR or multi-modal Vision LLMs. Vision models introduce network latency, incur per-call API costs, are non-deterministic, and fail on dynamic viewports.","naive":[{"t":"Multi-Modal Vision LLMs (GPT-4o / Claude Vision)","n":["Non-deterministic; hallucinations cause false positives on gradient backgrounds and miss sub-pixel collisions on complex dashboards."]},{"t":"Canvas Pixel Diffing","n":["Pixel diffing requires a golden baseline image. It cannot audit arbitrary third-party websites without pre-existing reference screenshots."]},{"t":"Naive CSS Color Parsing","n":["Checking `getComputedStyle(el).backgroundColor` returns `rgba(0,0,0,0)` (transparent) for most text nodes, failing standard WCAG contrast checks."]}],"arch":"1. **Recursive Parent Luminance Bubbling:** If an element has transparent background, the analyzer traverses up the DOM tree until it resolves a non-transparent ancestor, applying the W3C relative luminance formula:\n   `L = 0.2126 * R + 0.7152 * G + 0.0722 * B` (with sRGB gamma expansion).\n2. **Axis-Aligned Bounding Box (AABB) 2D Intersection:** Evaluates 2D rectangle overlaps across `fixed`, `sticky`, and `absolute` elements in Chrome memory.\n3. **Ancestry Exclusion Invariant:** Checks `!a.contains(b) && !b.contains(a)` to ensure parent containers housing child elements are never falsely flagged as collisions.\n4. **Local DOM Evaluation:** Computes all calculations directly inside Chrome's V8 engine via `page.evaluate()` without sending screenshots or prompt tokens to external models.","verify":"","src":"src/geometry_analyzer.js"},{"slug":"in-browser-resource-timing-memory-extractor","q":"How to extract third-party network weight directly from browser memory without TLS interception proxies?","problem":"Auditing a web page's total network footprint and third-party script bloat usually requires running an external HTTP man-in-the-middle (MITM) proxy daemon (like mitmproxy or browsermob-proxy). Intercepting and buffering all TLS traffic outside the browser introduces massive CPU overhead, SSL re-encryption latency, and packet buffering memory consumption.","naive":[{"t":"External MITM Proxy Daemons","n":["Require installing custom root CA certificates inside Chromium and buffering gigabytes of network packets in proxy RAM."]},{"t":"CDP Network Domain Event Buffering","n":["Subscribing to Chrome DevTools Protocol `Network.responseReceived` emits tens of thousands of WebSocket events over IPC, causing Node.js event loop lag."]},{"t":"CORS and Origin Obfuscation","n":["Naive HTTP log parsing fails to accurately associate dynamic CDN sub-resources with the true host origin."]}],"arch":"1. **Direct V8 Memory Inspection:** Evaluates `performance.getEntriesByType('resource')` inside the browser's JavaScript execution thread. Browser engines (V8/Blink) maintain low-level network performance counters natively in memory.\n2. **Zero-Copy Byte Aggregation:** Extracts `transferSize || encodedBodySize` directly from native `PerformanceResourceTiming` objects without streaming HTTP chunks across process boundaries.\n3. **In-Browser Origin Partitioning:** Compares `new URL(resource.name).origin !== location.origin`, accurately tallying third-party tracking, analytics, and CDN weight in sub-millisecond execution time.\n4. **Resilient Error Swallowing:** Wraps URL parsing in a fast try-catch closure to cleanly absorb invalid browser resource pseudo-URIs (such as `blob:`, `data:`, or malformed extension schemes).\n\n```javascript\n// 1. Extract resource transfer and encoded body metrics directly from browser memory\nconst resources = performance.getEntriesByType('resource').map((entry) => ({\n    name: entry.name,\n    bytes: entry.transferSize || entry.encodedBodySize || 0\n}));\n\n// 2. Compute third-party byte distribution against page origin\nconst origin = location.origin;\nlet thirdPartyBytes = 0;\nfor (const resource of resources) {\n    try {\n        if (new URL(resource.name).origin !== origin) thirdPartyBytes += resource.bytes;\n    } catch { /* ignore invalid browser entries */ }\n}\n```","verify":"","src":"src/resource_timing_extractor.js"},{"slug":"keyset-pagination-cursor-credential-scrubbing","q":"How to eliminate linear OFFSET query degradation and scrub URL credentials in support ticketing ledgers?","problem":"In customer support and ticketing backends, two problems frequently occur:\n1. **Offset Pagination Degradation:** Using `OFFSET 10000` causes linear performance degradation and full table scans. Furthermore, concurrent ticket creation shifts row offsets, causing users to see duplicate items or miss newly created tickets.\n2. **Credential Leaks in User Bug Reports:** Users copying and pasting internal URLs into support tickets often paste authenticated URLs containing embedded credentials (`https://{user}:{password}@internal.corp/debug`). Storing these in plain text risks exposing sensitive internal passwords to support staff and logging pipelines.","naive":[{"t":"Traditional Limit/Offset Queries","n":["Scanning 50 pages of tickets forces the database engine to traverse and discard thousands of rows for every page turn."]},{"t":"Unvalidated Cursor Payloads","n":["Passing unverified cursor strings allows malicious clients to inject malformed JSON, crash API endpoints, or probe unauthorized partition bounds."]},{"t":"Naive String URL Storage","n":["Storing raw user strings without protocol and credential sanitization stores plaintext basic auth secrets in audit databases."]}],"arch":"1. **Base64url Encoded Keyset Cursors:** Serializes deterministic state tuples (`{ at, id }`) into compact Base64url tokens. `decodeCursor()` bounds token length to 512 bytes, verifies JSON structure, and asserts finite ISO timestamp parsing.\n2. **Index Seek (O(log n)) Instead of Offset Scan:** Uses indexed compound filters (`WHERE (created_at, id) < ($1, $2)`), executing an index seek instead of scanning and discarding preceding rows with OFFSET regardless of table depth.\n3. **URL Protocol & Credential Sanitization:** Parses input via native `URL`. Strictly rejects non-HTTP(S) schemes and asserts `!parsed.username && !parsed.password`. Authenticated URLs with embedded credentials are discarded rather than persisted.\n\n```javascript\n// 1. Keyset pagination cursor encoding and strict decoding\nfunction encodeCursor(value) {\n    return Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(value), 'utf8').toString('base64url');\n}\n\nfunction decodeCursor(value) {\n    if (!value) return null;\n    if (typeof value !== 'string' || value.length > 512) {\n        throw new AppError('The support pagination cursor is invalid.', { status: 400, code: 'SUPPORT_CURSOR_INVALID' });\n    }\n    const decoded = JSON.parse(Buffer.from(value, 'base64url').toString('utf8'));\n    if (!decoded?.id || !Number.isFinite(Date.parse(decoded.at))) throw new Error('invalid');\n    return { at: new Date(decoded.at).toISOString(), id: decoded.id };\n}\n\n// 2. Structured message context extraction scrubbing embedded credentials from URLs\ntry {\n    const parsed = fields.targeturl ? new URL(fields.targeturl) : null;\n    if (parsed && ['http:', 'https:'].includes(parsed.protocol) && !parsed.username && !parsed.password) {\n        targetUrl = parsed.toString();\n    }\n} catch { /* malformed context is ignored */ }\n```","verify":"","src":"src/support_keyset_cursor.js"},{"slug":"reactive-signal-dependency-pruning","q":"How to eliminate zombie computations and memory leaks in fine-grained signals via dynamic dependency pruning?","problem":"In fine-grained reactive runtimes (such as Solid, Svelte, or Vue), an effect that evaluates dynamic conditional branches (`condition() ? signalA() : signalB()`) subscribes to whichever signal is read. If `condition` later flips from `true` to `false`, naive subscription graphs keep the effect permanently registered in `signalA`'s subscriber set. Subsequent updates to `signalA` continue triggering the effect unnecessarily (Zombie Computation), leaking memory and executing stale work.","naive":[{"t":"Static Dependency Registration","n":["Subscribing once during initialization fails to reflect changing branches, causing effects to execute on irrelevant state mutations."]},{"t":"Memory Leakage Across Component Trees","n":["Retaining references to unread signals prevents garbage collection of detached DOM nodes and parent scopes."]},{"t":"Infinite Re-Entrant Loops","n":["If an effect updates a signal it inadvertently reads, missing recursion guards (`running`) cause fatal call stack overflow crashes."]}],"arch":"1. **Pre-Execution Subscription Disposal:** Before re-evaluating `fn()`, the effect traverses `dependencies` (a `Set<Set<() => void>>`) and deletes itself from every signal's subscriber set, then calls `dependencies.clear()`.\n2. **Fresh Dependency Recording:** As `fn()` executes under `setActiveEffect(effectFn)`, only signals actually evaluated in the active branch add `effectFn` back to their subscriber sets.\n3. **Deterministic Cleanup Hook:** Executes the previous execution's returned cleanup callback before re-running, ensuring timers, DOM listeners, or abort signals are torn down cleanly.\n4. **Recursion Lock:** Enforces `let running = false` to prevent an effect from triggering itself in an infinite re-entrant cycle.\n\n```typescript\n// 1. Cycle prevention and user cleanup execution\nif (!active || running) return;\nrunning = true;\nif (cleanup) {\n  const previousCleanup = cleanup;\n  cleanup = undefined;\n  previousCleanup();\n}\n\n// 2. Dynamic dependency pruning: clear stale signal subscriptions before re-run\nif ((effectFn as any).dependencies) {\n  for (const subscribers of (effectFn as any).dependencies) {\n    subscribers.delete(effectFn);\n  }\n  (effectFn as any).dependencies.clear();\n}\n\n// 3. Re-record active subscriptions during execution\nconst previousEffect = activeEffect;\nsetActiveEffect(effectFn);\ntry {\n  const result = fn();\n  cleanup = typeof result === 'function' ? result : undefined;\n} finally {\n  setActiveEffect(previousEffect);\n  running = false;\n}\n```","verify":"","src":"src/effect_pruning.ts"},{"slug":"proxyless-reactive-store-descriptors","q":"How to eliminate Proxy trap overhead in reactive stores using Object.defineProperty descriptors and auto-syncing storage?","problem":"Global state management libraries often rely on ES6 `Proxy` objects or Redux-style action dispatchers with reducers. ES6 Proxies add property lookup overhead and trap performance penalties across thousands of reads in hot loops. Meanwhile, Redux-style immutable stores require repetitive action creators and full object cloning on every mutation.","naive":[{"t":"Proxy Trap Performance Tax","n":["Intercepting every property access through deep Proxy handlers slows down execution in render loops."]},{"t":"Boilerplate Reducer Overhead","n":["Dispatching actions through reducers creates unnecessary object cloning and event loop churn."]},{"t":"Disconnected Storage Persistence","n":["Syncing state to `localStorage` or `sessionStorage` typically requires third-party middleware prone to state desynchronization."]}],"arch":"1. **Signal-Backed Property Map:** Each state property maps to an isolated `createSignal()` tuple (`[get, set]`).\n2. **Dynamic Context Descriptors:** `Object.defineProperty(context, key, ...)` defines getters and setters on the action `this` context:\n   * Reading `this.count` calls `get()`.\n   * Mutating `this.count = 5` calls `store.setCount(5)`.\n3. **Direct Property Descriptors (No Proxy):** Operations execute as direct function calls and native V8 property lookups without proxy trapping.\n4. **Integrated Storage Persistence:** When `persist: 'local' | 'session'` is configured, setter invocations automatically serialize state snapshots to web storage.\n\n```typescript\n// 1. Setup fine-grained signals for each store state key\nfor (const key in initialState) {\n  const [get, set] = createSignal(initialState[key]);\n  stateSignals[key] = [get, set];\n  storeInstance[key] = get;\n\n  const capitalizedKey = key.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + key.slice(1);\n  storeInstance[`set${capitalizedKey}`] = (val: any) => {\n    set(val);\n    if (config.persist) persistState.save(config.name, getStoreSnapshot(), config.persist);\n  };\n}\n\n// 2. Bind action execution contexts with dynamic property descriptors\nif (config.actions) {\n  const context: any = {};\n  for (const key in initialState) {\n    Object.defineProperty(context, key, {\n      get: () => stateSignals[key][0](),\n      set: (val) => storeInstance[`set${key.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + key.slice(1)}`](val),\n    });\n  }\n\n  for (const actionName in config.actions) {\n    storeInstance[actionName] = (...args: any[]) => {\n      return config.actions![actionName].apply(context, args);\n    };\n  }\n}\n```","verify":"","src":"src/reactive_store.ts"},{"slug":"bidirectional-reactive-dependency-tracking","q":"How to eliminate zombie subscriptions and recursion cycles in fine-grained signals via bidirectional set tracking?","problem":"1. **Zombie Subscriptions in Conditional Branches:** In reactive runtimes, evaluating ternary branches (`cond() ? a() : b()`) leaves stale subscriptions in dependencies that are no longer active, leaking memory.\n2. **Infinite Recursion on Self-Mutating Effects:** If an effect writes to a signal that it also reads (`count(count() + 1)`), standard observers enter infinite recursion and exhaust the call stack.\n3. **Missing Teardown Lifecycles:** Effects subscribing to external WebSockets, DOM event listeners, or timers require reliable execution of returned cleanup functions before re-evaluation or disposal.","naive":[{"t":"One-Way Subscription Arrays","n":["Storing effects strictly on signal subscriber lists prevents the effect from knowing which signals to detach from when its execution path branches."]},{"t":"Naive Re-Entry Flags","n":["Global boolean execution flags prevent any effect from triggering updates, halting unrelated state transitions across concurrent component boundaries."]},{"t":"Manual Subscription Management","n":["Forcing developers to manually dispose listeners introduces memory leaks and boilerplate."]}],"arch":"1. **Bidirectional Dependency Tracking (`deps: Set<Set<ReactiveEffect>>`):** The effect holds a reference to every signal's subscriber set. Prior to each run, it deletes itself from every tracked dependency set and clears its internal list.\n2. **Per-Runner Recursion Guard (`runner.running = true`):** Restricts recursion protection strictly to the active runner instance, avoiding global reactive pipeline freezes while preventing infinite self-invocation.\n3. **Clean Teardown Guarantees:** Returns from effect functions are captured as `userCleanup` and executed before subsequent runs and upon unmount.","verify":"","src":"src/effect_tracking.ts"},{"slug":"audio-dsp-biquad-slew-rate-smoothing","q":"How to eliminate digital popping and audio driver clicks in real-time 10-band equalizers via slew-rate smoothing?","problem":"1. **Digital Audio Clicks and Popping:** When users adjust equalizer sliders in desktop music players, abruptly updating IIR filter coefficients mid-stream produces discontinuous waveform jumps that sound like loud static clicks and can damage speakers.\n2. **CPU Overload from Per-Sample Coefficient Math:** Recalculating trigonometric biquad filter transfer functions (`sin`, `cos`, `alpha`) on every single audio sample (44,100 times per second) exhausts CPU cores.\n3. **Digital Clipping from Positive Gain Boosts:** Boosting low frequencies (e.g. +12 dB bass) amplifies samples beyond the [-1.0, 1.0] float range, causing harsh digital clipping distortion.","naive":[{"t":"Instantaneous Coefficient Replacement","n":["Directly overwriting biquad delay buffers (`x1`, `x2`, `y1`, `y2`) creates instant step discontinuities in the output signal."]},{"t":"Sample-Rate Coefficient Smoothing","n":["Calculating filter pole-zero placement on every sample introduces floating-point division and trig bottlenecks in the critical audio path."]},{"t":"Fixed Preamp Attenuation","n":["Applying global volume reduction even when EQ gains are flat reduces signal dynamic range unnecessarily."]}],"arch":"1. **Frame-Bounded Decimation (`128-Frame Block Rate`):** Coefficient interpolation runs strictly once every 128 audio frames (`44,100 / 128 ≈ 344 updates/s`, `~2.9ms` at 44.1kHz), reducing trigonometric calculation frequency by `1 - 1/128 ≈ 99.2%`.\n2. **Geometric Slew-Rate Smoothing (`12% Convergence`):** Gains interpolate geometrically toward user targets (`*current += (target - *current) * 0.12`), ensuring seamless analog-like transitions without audible pops.\n3. **Dynamic Preamp Compensation:** Computes peak negative gain offset (`eq_preamp`), automatically attenuating the input stage to preserve dynamic headroom and prevent clipping.","verify":"The decimation and update rates are derived directly from the standard audio PCM sampling rate and processing buffer geometry:\n\n1. **Update Frequency ($F_{\\text{update}}$):**\n   $$\\text{Updates per second} = \\frac{f_s}{B} = \\frac{44,100\\text{ samples/s}}{128\\text{ frames}} = 344.53125\\text{ updates/s} \\approx 344\\text{ updates/s}$$\n\n2. **Block Period / Update Interval ($T_{\\text{block}}$):**\n   $$T_{\\text{block}} = \\frac{128}{44,100}\\text{ s} \\approx 0.0029025\\text{ s} \\approx 2.90\\text{ ms}$$\n\n3. **Trigonometric Calculation Reduction:**\n   $$\\text{Computation Reduction} = 1 - \\frac{1}{B} = 1 - \\frac{1}{128} = \\frac{127}{128} \\approx 99.21875\\% \\approx 99.2\\%$$\n\n4. **Geometric Convergence Step:**\n   $$G_{k+1} = G_k + 0.12 \\times (G_{\\text{target}} - G_k)$$\n   At $\\sim 344.5\\text{ updates/s}$, an instantaneous 10 dB slider step settles within $\\sim 15\\text{ to }20\\text{ blocks}$ ($\\sim 45\\text{ to }60\\text{ ms}$), well beneath human acoustic perception of discrete steps while preventing waveform discontinuities.","src":"src/biquad_slew_equalizer.rs"},{"slug":"semver-versionless-packagemanager-guard","q":"How to prevent fatal SemVer parsing crashes in dependency upgrade engines on Corepack versionless packageManager fields?","problem":"1. **Crash on Corepack Bare Declarations:** Node.js Corepack and modern package managers allow bare declarations like `\"packageManager\": \"pnpm\"` or `\"packageManager\": \"yarn\"` without an `@version` suffix in `package.json`.\n2. **Fatal TypeError in SemVer Upgrade Loops:** Dependency upgrade utilities (like `npm-check-updates`) splitting by `@` received `undefined` for the version token, passing it into SemVer comparison functions that threw unhandled exceptions and crashed the CLI tool.\n3. **Accidental Modification of User Configuration:** Failing to account for versionless fields caused parsers to overwrite or delete valid user configuration.","naive":[{"t":"Unchecked String Splitting","n":["Assuming `packageManager` always adheres to the `name@version` specification."]},{"t":"Throwing on Unsupported Syntax","n":["Crashing the entire dependency upgrade process for the repository because of an optional tooling field."]},{"t":"Regex Extraction without Fallbacks","n":["Using strict regular expressions that fail to match bare names, leaving the dependency in an undefined state."]}],"arch":"1. **Safe Conditional Mapping:** `parsePackageManager` splits the string and verifies that `version` is truthy before returning a dependency entry.\n2. **Preservation of Existing Fields:** If no version is specified, an empty dictionary is returned, allowing `npm-check-updates` to continue upgrading all regular dependencies without modifying or corrupting the bare `packageManager` entry.\n3. **End-to-End Regression Test Suite:** Validated via Vitest suites ensuring `packageManager: \"pnpm\"` remains intact while `dependencies` upgrade seamlessly.","verify":"","src":"src/package_manager_parser.ts"},{"slug":"yaml-literal-block-scalar-type-preservation","q":"How to preserve YAML 1.2 literal block scalars as strings during unquoted type deserialization?","problem":"1. **YAML 1.2 Specification Violation:** Under the YAML 1.2 specification, literal block scalars (denoted by the pipe symbol `|` or `|-`) represent formatted text blocks and must strictly be treated as strings.\n2. **Unintended Type Coercion:** When deserializing dynamic YAML documents with `WithAttemptingUnquotedStringTypeDeserialization`, block scalars containing values like `true`, `false`, `null`, or numbers were erroneously converted into C# booleans, null references, or integers.\n3. **Data Loss in Multiline Configuration:** Configuration templates containing scripts, certificates, or text blocks lost formatting and type integrity when parsed as dynamic dictionaries.","naive":[{"t":"Exclusionary Style Whitelisting","n":["Checking `value.Style == SingleQuoted || DoubleQuoted || Folded` forgot `ScalarStyle.Literal`."]},{"t":"Post-Deserialization String Casting","n":["Forcing callers to cast objects back to strings fails if the parser has already mutated `\"true\"` into boolean `true` or `\"null\"` into an empty reference."]},{"t":"Disabling Type Inference Globally","n":["Turning off type deserialization breaks expected parsing of unquoted numbers and booleans across the rest of the document."]}],"arch":"1. **Inversion to Plain-Scalar Filtering (`value.Style != ScalarStyle.Plain`):** In `ScalarNodeDeserializer.cs`, the condition was inverted. Type inference is strictly restricted to plain scalars. Any scalar with explicit formatting (quoted, folded, or literal block) is preserved directly as a string.\n2. **Comprehensive Theory Tests:** Validated via xUnit `[Theory]` test cases testing empty blocks (`Value: |-`), boolean-like blocks (`Value: |-\\n  true`), null blocks, and numeric blocks.\n3. **Single Comparison Instruction:** Eliminates multiple style equality checks by executing a single inequality comparison (`value.Style != ScalarStyle.Plain`).","verify":"","src":"src/yaml_scalar_deserializer.cs"},{"slug":"depth-balanced-sfc-sticky-parser","q":"How to lex custom Single File Components without regex corruption using depth-balanced parsing and sticky attribute scanning?","problem":"Single File Component (SFC) compilers must parse templates containing complex JavaScript expressions (`{items.map(x => ({ val: x }))}`), nested quotes inside HTML attributes (`data-label=\"a > b\"`), and structural directives (`{#if}`, `{#each}`). Naive regular expression parsers break on nested braces, truncate attributes at unescaped greater-than symbols, or suffer from catastrophic regex backtracking (ReDoS).","naive":[{"t":"Regex Greedy Attribute Matching","n":["`/<[^>]+>/` terminates prematurely on `data-condition=\"a > b\"`, splitting HTML tags in half."]},{"t":"Regex Expression Truncation","n":["`/\\{([^\\}]+)\\}/` stops at the first closing curly brace, mangling object literals inside expressions (`{ { a: 1 } }`)."]},{"t":"Catastrophic Backtracking (ReDoS)","n":["Unbounded whitespace repetitions in attribute regexes crash build workers when parsing long SVG paths or multi-line attributes."]}],"arch":"1. **Depth-Balanced Expression Scanner:** `findExpressionEnd()` maintains an integer depth counter (`depth += 1 / depth -= 1`) while tracking string quotation boundaries (`\"`, `'`, `` ` ``). It returns only when `depth === 0` outside any string literal.\n2. **Sticky Attribute Scanning (`/gy`):** Uses the sticky `y` regex flag with explicit `lastIndex = cursor`. Matches advance contiguously across source characters in O(N) time without catastrophic regex backtracking.\n3. **Structured Directive Separation:** Automatically classifies attributes by prefix:\n   * `@event` -> Event handlers (`@click=\"save\"`).\n   * `:binding` -> Reactive bindings (`:class=\"active\"`).\n   * Plain attributes -> Static HTML attributes.\n4. **HTML Void Tag Invariance:** Tracks standard void elements (`img`, `br`, `input`, `hr`) to prevent unclosed tag validation errors on self-closing HTML.\n\n```typescript\n// 1. Depth-balanced expression end scanning handling nested curly braces and quotes\nfunction findExpressionEnd(source: string, start: number): number {\n  let depth = 0;\n  let quote = '';\n  for (let index = start; index < source.length; index += 1) {\n    const char = source[index];\n    if (quote) {\n      if (char === quote && source[index - 1] !== '\\\\') quote = '';\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (char === '\"' || char === \"'\" || char === '`') quote = char;\n    else if (char === '{') depth += 1;\n    else if (char === '}') {\n      depth -= 1;\n      if (depth === 0) return index;\n    }\n  }\n  return -1;\n}\n\n// 2. Sticky regex (/gy) attribute scanner eliminating catastrophic regex backtracking\nfunction parseAttributes(source: string, filename: string) {\n  const attributes: Record<string, string> = {};\n  const events: Record<string, string> = {};\n  const bindings: Record<string, string> = {};\n  const expr = /\\s*([@:])?([A-Za-z_][\\w:-]*)(?:\\s*=\\s*(?:\"([^\"]*)\"|'([^']*)'))?/gy;\n  let cursor = 0;\n\n  while (cursor < source.length) {\n    expr.lastIndex = cursor;\n    const match = expr.exec(source);\n    if (!match) break;\n    cursor = expr.lastIndex;\n    const [_, prefix, name, v1, v2] = match;\n    const value = v1 ?? v2 ?? '';\n    if (prefix === '@') events[name] = value;\n    else if (prefix === ':') bindings[name] = value;\n    else attributes[name] = value;\n  }\n  return { attributes, events, bindings };\n}\n```","verify":"","src":"src/sfc_parser.ts"}],"dir":"04-memory-and-performance","blurb":"Zero-copy parsing, RAM reverse-scraping, GC mitigation"},{"n":5,"name":"Operating System & Process Management","cases":[{"slug":"chrome-sqlite-shadow-profile-sync","q":"How to clone authenticated Chrome sessions without SQLite file-lock crashes or terminating active user tabs?","problem":"Desktop AI browser automation fails on modern web applications because fresh browser sessions encounter Cloudflare bot detection, login walls, and SMS 2FA.\n\nHowever, launching a second Chrome instance with `--remote-debugging-port` pointed at the user's active Chrome profile fails with a fatal crash. Chrome places OS-level exclusive write locks on its SQLite databases (`Cookies`, `Login Data`, `Web Data`). Killing the user's active browser is intolerable as it destroys unsubmitted forms and active tabs.","naive":[{"t":"Process Termination (`killall chrome`)","n":["Destroys user productivity and active workspaces."]},{"t":"Standard Folder Copy (`cp -r User Data`)","n":["Fails due to multi-gigabyte cache directories, taking tens of seconds, and throws file-lock permission errors on locked WAL journal files."]}],"arch":"We engineered an Atomic Shadow Profile Sync in Rust:\n\n1. **Lock-Free Read Streaming:** While SQLite locks writing, the OS allows non-exclusive read operations on underlying database files.\n2. **Master DPAPI Key Re-encryption:** We copy `Local State` which contains the DPAPI-encrypted master key used to decrypt cookies.\n3. **Surgical Cloning:** We surgically clone only `Preferences`, `Secure Preferences`, and `Network/Cookies` into a temporary shadow profile directory.\n4. **Isolated Launch:** The AI launches against the lightweight shadow profile, instantly running with existing authenticated session cookies preserved.","verify":"","src":"src/shadow_profile.rs"},{"slug":"fixed-port-loopback-origin-preservation","q":"How to prevent Electron desktop SPAs from silently wiping IndexedDB and localStorage across launches using fixed loopback ports?","problem":"1. **Web Storage Partitioning Across Launches:** In Electron desktop apps serving Next.js or React SPAs, using dynamic ephemeral ports (`server.listen(0)`) changes the web origin (`http://127.0.0.1:XXXXX`) on every boot, isolating and wiping user `localStorage`, `IndexedDB`, and offline video caches.\n2. **File Protocol (`file://`) Security Limitations:** Loading exported SPAs via `file://` breaks HTML5 pushState routing, CORS for web workers, and WebAssembly video transcoding pipelines.\n3. **Port Collisions on Multi-App Desktops:** Hardcoding a fixed port without fail-safe error handling crashes the desktop application if another process occupies the port.","naive":[{"t":"Random Port Ephemeral Servers","n":["Binding to port 0 generates a clean origin on every launch, forcing users to re-login and losing unsaved project state."]},{"t":"Standard Custom Protocols (`app://`)","n":["Electron custom protocol handlers lack native stream piping, causing performance degradation when streaming multi-gigabyte video assets."]},{"t":"Unprotected Static Servers","n":["Listening on `0.0.0.0` exposes internal application files and APIs to other devices on the local Wi-Fi network."]}],"arch":"1. **Fixed Dedicated Production Port (`PRODUCTION_PORT = 38450`):** Binds strictly to `127.0.0.1:38450`, ensuring that the origin `http://127.0.0.1:38450` remains identical across every application session, preserving `IndexedDB` and `localStorage`.\n2. **Graceful `EADDRINUSE` Fallback:** Hooks `staticServer.once('error')` to dynamically fall back to an ephemeral port if another instance is already running, preventing crashes.\n3. **Surgical SPA Path Resolution:** Inspects requested URL paths and automatically resolves extensionless routes to `.html` or fallback `index.html`.","verify":"","src":"src/loopback_server.ts"},{"slug":"frameless-window-delta-drag-ipc","q":"How to implement frameless Electron window dragging without CSS drag regions hijacking DOM click events and context menus?","problem":"1. **CSS `-webkit-app-region: drag` Event Hijacking:** In custom frameless Electron desktop apps (`frame: false`), applying CSS drag regions breaks HTML DOM click handlers, text selection, buttons, and custom context menus on Windows.\n2. **Cursor Snapping Glitches on Maximized Windows:** Dragging a maximized window down from the top bar snaps the window violently to screen coordinates (0, 0) or freezes the mouse pointer.\n3. **Double-Click Titlebar Conflicts:** Drag regions intercept double-click events, preventing users from maximizing or restoring windows via standard OS titlebar gestures.","naive":[{"t":"Native CSS Drag Regions","n":["Electron's Chromium implementation routes native drag regions directly to Windows OS window manager hooks, completely bypassing the web renderer event loop."]},{"t":"Absolute Screen Positioning","n":["Directly setting `setPosition(screenX, screenY)` places the top-left corner of the window under the mouse cursor instead of preserving the grab offset."]},{"t":"Polling Mouse Coordinates","n":["Running `screen.getCursorScreenPoint()` on high-frequency timers burns background CPU and introduces lag."]}],"arch":"1. **Delta Offset Calculation (`screenX - dragStartPos.x`):** Captures initial mouse coordinates and window origin on `window-drag-start`. Incremental moves update coordinates relative to the initial offset, ensuring natural physics.\n2. **Atomic Unmaximize Coordinate Re-Anchoring:** When dragging a maximized window, `unmaximize()` is invoked, and the origin offsets are instantly reset to the cursor position to prevent teleportation.\n3. **Full Web Renderer Control:** All dragging occurs via lightweight IPC events, allowing web buttons, dropdowns, and search bars to remain fully interactive inside the custom titlebar.","verify":"","src":"src/window_dragging_ipc.ts"},{"slug":"windows-sqlite-profile-file-lock-race","q":"How to eliminate fatal EBUSY process crashes from asynchronous Windows SQLite profile file locks during Chrome teardown?","problem":"When running automated Lighthouse or headless Chromium audits in developer environments or CI/CD pipelines running on Windows, Chromium holds asynchronous OS-level file locks on internal SQLite database caches (`Cookies`, `Web Data`, `History`) for several milliseconds after process termination. Standard `chromeLauncher.kill()` attempts to synchronously delete the temporary profile directory (`destroyTmp`), immediately throwing an unhandled `EBUSY: resource busy or locked` exception and crashing the Node.js process.","naive":[{"t":"Windows vs POSIX File Locking Semantics","n":["On Linux, unlinking an open file succeeds (it is deleted once the file descriptor closes). On Windows, attempting to delete an open file or folder throws `EBUSY` immediately."]},{"t":"Synchronous Teardown in chrome-launcher","n":["`chromeLauncher` executes `destroyTmp` synchronously during `kill()`. Even if the Chrome process has received `SIGKILL`, SQLite file locks are released asynchronously by the Windows kernel."]},{"t":"Unhandled Promise Rejections in Worker Pools","n":["An unhandled synchronous throw inside `killChrome()` terminates worker threads, corrupting concurrent scan jobs."]}],"arch":"1. **Teardown Method Monkey-Patching:** Before calling `chrome.kill()`, the function inspects `chrome.destroyTmp`. It wraps the method in a protective try-catch closure, intercepting synchronous `EBUSY` and `EPERM` exceptions.\n2. **Double-Layered Catch Boundary:** Surrounds `await chrome.kill()` in a secondary try-catch block, ensuring that any residual Windows kernel lock races cannot bubble up to the worker execution loop.\n3. **Safe Temp Cleanup Delegation:** Allows the operating system's temporary directory cleaner or subsequent process boots to clean residual profile directories without crashing active audit threads.\n\n```javascript\n// Monkey-patching chromeLauncher.destroyTmp to prevent fatal EBUSY crash on Windows\nasync function killChrome(chrome) {\n    if (!chrome) return;\n    if (typeof chrome.destroyTmp === 'function') {\n        const destroyTmp = chrome.destroyTmp;\n        chrome.destroyTmp = () => {\n            try { destroyTmp.call(chrome); }\n            catch { /* chrome-launcher races locked Windows SQLite profile caches */ }\n        };\n    }\n    try {\n        await chrome.kill();\n    } catch {\n        // chrome-launcher can throw synchronously while cleaning temporary profiles\n        // on Windows even after the Chrome process has exited.\n    }\n}\n```","verify":"","src":"src/chrome_teardown_patch.js"}],"dir":"05-os-and-process-architecture","blurb":"Subprocess lifecycle, SQLite file-lock bypasses, DPAPI"},{"n":6,"name":"Autonomous Agents & Automation","cases":[{"slug":"deterministic-intent-macros-vs-llm-loops","q":"How to reduce LLM round-trips using atomic deterministic intent macros instead of brute-force loops?","problem":"Standard autonomous browser agent frameworks (Browser-Use, Agent-E, generic LangChain tools) interact with websites via brute-force single-step loops:\n* Step 1: LLM locates the search input.\n* Step 2: LLM types the query.\n* Step 3: LLM presses enter.\n* Step 4: LLM identifies the search results.\n* Step 5: LLM selects the desired link.\n\nThis introduces cumulative network latency across multi-turn reasoning loops, burns model tokens on repeated DOM dumps, and creates multiple separate opportunities for reasoning failures or DOM selector breakdowns.","naive":[{"t":"Brute-Force Prompt Chaining","n":["Each turn incurs LLM network roundtrips, context token reprocessing, and vision screenshot encoding overhead."]},{"t":"Fragile Exact CSS Selectors","n":["Modern single-page applications (YouTube, Google, React/Vue portals) change element class names and IDs on every build. Hardcoded `#search-box` queries break frequently."]}],"arch":"We replaced the 5-turn loop with an Atomic Client-Side Intent Macro (`searchAndSelect`):\n\n1. **Single High-Level Intent:** The LLM issues one atomic instruction: `search_and_select(query, index: 0)`.\n2. **Platform-Aware Heuristic Tree:** The client-side runtime automatically determines target platform inputs (YouTube search box, Google query bar, standard form inputs).\n3. **Geometric Visibility Filtering:** Queries candidates with prioritized fallback selectors and filters them via `getBoundingClientRect()` and `offsetParent !== null` to guarantee element visibility before interaction.\n4. **Single-Evaluation Execution:** In a single browser evaluation tick, the query is typed, dispatched via `requestSubmit()`, scrolled into viewport center, and clicked deterministically.","verify":"","src":"src/intent_macro.ts"},{"slug":"crawler-trap-and-combinatorial-explosion-defense","q":"How to neutralize crawler traps, infinite calendars, and combinatorial query explosions with bounded limits?","problem":"Automated website crawlers face adversarial website architectures: infinite calendar widgets (clicking \"Next Year\" into infinity), faceted eCommerce search filters generating factorial query permutations (`?color=red&size=m&sort=asc...`), and deep cyclic URL structures. Without explicit boundaries, crawlers run out of memory or DDoS target hosts.","naive":[{"t":"Simple URL Visited Sets","n":["Tracking visited URLs in a Set fails because dynamic parameters (`?page=1000`, `?date=2040`) generate millions of unique strings for identical pages."]},{"t":"Infinite Sitemap Recursion","n":["Malicious or recursive sitemap indexes nested 50 levels deep exhaust memory or trigger gzip decompression bombs."]},{"t":"Unbounded URL Queues","n":["Allowing queues to grow without limits causes memory leaks and starves container worker pools."]}],"arch":"1. **Calendar Year Drift Clamping:** Rejects calendar drift beyond `maxCalendarYearDrift: 5`, immediately severing infinite calendar date click loops.\n2. **Path Depth & Combinatorial Query Ceilings:** Restricts maximum URL path depth to 16 and clamps query parameter variants per unique path to 5, killing faceted search explosions.\n3. **Pagination Upper Bounds:** Imposes a strict ceiling on pagination numbers (`maxPaginationValue: 250`) preventing crawlers from crawling arbitrarily large pagination sequences.\n4. **Sitemap Decompression & Depth Fencing:** Caps sitemap decompression at 8 MB and nesting depth at 3, neutralizing XML expansion bombs.","verify":"","src":"src/crawler_limits.js"},{"slug":"browser-synthetic-journey-sandbox-route-interception","q":"How to build a fail-closed synthetic browser journey sandbox with network route interception and click mutation guards?","problem":"Executing automated synthetic user journeys against live customer websites introduces catastrophic safety risks: synthetic scripts can inadvertently click form submit buttons that process real financial transactions, delete production data, or navigate to malicious cross-origin URLs.","naive":[{"t":"Standard Playwright / Puppeteer Scripts","n":["Blindly execute `page.click()` on any selector, triggering unwanted POST mutations or form submissions."]},{"t":"Relying on Target Web App Safeguards","n":["Third-party applications under test rarely have safe staging boundaries, meaning test clicks execute real production side-effects."]},{"t":"Unfiltered Network Requests","n":["Subresources, third-party trackers, and tracking pixels can execute side-channel mutations or leak session state during automated runs."]}],"arch":"1. **Fail-Closed Network Route Interception:** Intercepts all browser network traffic via `page.route('**/*', ...)`. Restricts requests strictly to `GET`, `HEAD`, and `OPTIONS`. Any mutating method (`POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`) or cross-origin destination is immediately aborted with `blockedbyclient`.\n2. **DOM-Level Click Safety Assertions:** Before clicking any element, evaluates its properties directly in browser DOM memory:\n   * Anchor tags (`<a>`) must strictly match the current origin (`new URL(el.href).origin === location.origin`).\n   * Buttons (`<button>`) must explicitly be `type=\"button\"`. Any `type=\"submit\"` button is strictly blocked.\n3. **Bounded Journey Schema Validation:** Validates journey contracts before execution: steps are capped between 2 and 20, step timeouts are bounded between 50ms and 15,000ms, and actions are strictly restricted to `goto`, `click`, `expectVisible`, `expectText`, and `waitFor`.\n\n```javascript\n// 1. Fail-closed network route interception: block mutations & cross-origin subresources\nawait page.route('**/*', async (route) => {\n    const request = route.request();\n    let allowed = ['GET', 'HEAD', 'OPTIONS'].includes(request.method());\n    try { allowed = allowed && new URL(request.url()).origin === origin; }\n    catch { allowed = false; }\n\n    if (allowed) await route.continue();\n    else {\n        blockedRequests.push({ method: request.method(), url: request.url() });\n        await route.abort('blockedbyclient');\n    }\n});\n\n// 2. DOM-level click mutation guard rejecting form submit buttons & external links\nif (step.action === 'click') {\n    const target = page.locator(step.selector).first();\n    const safe = await target.evaluate((el) =>\n        el.tagName === 'A'\n            ? new URL(el.href).origin === location.origin\n            : el.tagName === 'BUTTON' && (el.type || 'submit') === 'button'\n    );\n    if (!safe) throw new Error('Only same-origin links and non-submit buttons are allowed.');\n    await target.click({ timeout: step.timeoutMs || 10_000 });\n}\n```","verify":"","src":"src/journey_sandbox.js"}],"dir":"06-autonomous-agents-and-automation","blurb":"Atomic intent macros, deterministic DOM geometry"},{"n":7,"name":"Fintech & Billing Invariants","cases":[{"slug":"partial-unique-index-checkout-idempotency","q":"How to prevent duplicate checkout intents and guarantee audit log atomicity at the database schema layer?","problem":"Double-clicks on checkout buttons or multiple open browser tabs spawn duplicate payment sessions and race conditions with providers like Paddle or Stripe. Furthermore, in high-compliance billing, if an audit log write fails, leaving an entitlement change committed creates severe compliance violations.","naive":[{"t":"Frontend Button Disabling","n":["Trivial to bypass via network retries, script injection, or opening two browser tabs simultaneously."]},{"t":"Full Table Unique Constraints","n":["A simple `UNIQUE(workspace_id)` prevents a customer from ever buying another subscription in the future after their first plan expires."]},{"t":"Decoupled Audit Logging","n":["Asynchronous or non-transactional audit logging allows database disks or network partitions to drop audit events while leaving privileged billing mutations committed."]}],"arch":"1. **Partial Unique Index Invariant:** `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ON wpa_checkout_acceptances(workspace_id) WHERE status IN ('accepted', 'checkout_created')` ensures only one active checkout intent can exist per tenant at any time.\n2. **Setup Lease Fencing:** Provider checkout creation is guarded by a bounded lease (`setup_lease_owner`, `setup_lease_token`), serializing external API calls while permitting crash recovery after timeout.\n3. **Mandatory Audit Atomicity:** Every entitlement and plan mutation is executed within a database transaction where audit log insertion is non-optional. If `wpa_audit_log` fails, the entire mutation immediately issues `ROLLBACK`.","verify":"","src":"src/checkout_idempotency.sql"},{"slug":"timing-safe-webhook-replay-fencing","q":"How to prevent billing webhook timing attacks and out-of-order state regression with replay fencing?","problem":"Webhooks from billing providers (Paddle, Stripe) are vulnerable to timing attacks when verified with standard string comparisons, allowing attackers to deduce valid signatures. Furthermore, lack of timestamp replay fencing enables replay attacks where valid historic payment events are re-sent to grant unauthorized subscriptions.","naive":[{"t":"Standard Equality Operators (`=== / ==`)","n":["Early-exit string comparisons terminate on the first mismatched byte, leaking cryptographic signature byte values through nanosecond timing discrepancies."]},{"t":"Missing or Loose Timestamp Windows","n":["Many handlers only check signature validity without timestamp bounds, allowing captured historical webhooks to be replayed days or weeks later."]},{"t":"Out-of-Order Delivery Race Conditions","n":["Network lag can deliver an older `subscription.canceled` webhook *after* a newer `subscription.resumed` webhook, mistakenly canceling an active paid subscription."]}],"arch":"1. **Constant-Time Verification:** Compares cryptographic signature buffers strictly using `crypto.timingSafeEqual()`, eliminating side-channel timing leaks.\n2. **5-Second Replay Fence:** Enforces `Math.abs(currentSeconds - timestamp) > 5` to reject any captured or delayed webhook outside an immediate 5-second window.\n3. **Monotonic Event Sequencing:** The billing engine records `occurred_at` timestamps and verifies that older events cannot regress newer subscription state.","verify":"","src":"src/webhook_verifier.js"},{"slug":"append-only-commercial-plan-ledger-immutability-trigger","q":"How to enforce immutable commercial plan change ledgers using PostgreSQL engine triggers?","problem":"In multi-tenant SaaS platforms, commercial plan changes (upgrades, downgrades, cancellations) are often applied as direct row mutations (`UPDATE workspaces SET plan_id = ...`). If a database admin accidentally mutates records or a billing race condition occurs, historical subscription state is lost, making financial audits and disputes impossible to resolve.","naive":[{"t":"Direct In-Place Column Updates","n":["Overwriting `plan_id` destroys historical billing provenance. There is no cryptographic proof of who authorized a change, when it occurred, or what the preceding plan was."]},{"t":"Application-Layer Audit Logging Alone","n":["If application code fails to write to an audit table or a developer modifies rows directly via `psql`, application-layer audit mechanisms are completely bypassed."]},{"t":"Duplicate Billing Webhooks","n":["Payment provider webhook retries can execute state transitions out of sequence without strict idempotency constraints."]}],"arch":"1. **Append-Only Transition Ledger:** Every commercial plan change is recorded in `wpa_user_plan_changes` with `before_plan_id`, `after_plan_id`, `source` (`admin`, `provider`, `redeem`, `system`), `reason`, and `created_by`.\n2. **Database Engine Immutability Trigger:** Enforces immutability at the PostgreSQL engine level via a `BEFORE UPDATE OR DELETE` trigger that raises SQLSTATE `55000` (`WPA_USER_PLAN_CHANGES_APPEND_ONLY`). Even a superuser executing an accidental `DELETE` is stopped by the database engine.\n3. **Per-User Idempotency Constraint:** Imposes `UNIQUE (user_id, idempotency_key)`, ensuring duplicate provider webhook dispatches or retried administrative API requests cannot create duplicate billing mutations.\n4. **Foreign Key Deletion Restrictions:** Configures `ON DELETE RESTRICT` on user and plan references, preventing orphaned billing histories.\n\n```sql\n-- 1. Dedicated append-only commercial transition ledger\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS wpa_user_plan_changes (\n    id text PRIMARY KEY,\n    user_id text NOT NULL REFERENCES \"user\"(id) ON DELETE RESTRICT,\n    before_plan_id text REFERENCES wpa_plan_catalog(id) ON DELETE RESTRICT,\n    after_plan_id text NOT NULL REFERENCES wpa_plan_catalog(id) ON DELETE RESTRICT,\n    source text NOT NULL DEFAULT 'admin' CHECK(source IN ('admin','provider','redeem','system','legacy')),\n    reason text NOT NULL,\n    created_by text NOT NULL,\n    idempotency_key text NOT NULL,\n    created_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n    UNIQUE(user_id, idempotency_key)\n);\n\n-- 2. Database engine level immutability trigger blocking UPDATE & DELETE\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION wpa_user_plan_changes_immutable() RETURNS trigger\nLANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$\nBEGIN\n    RAISE EXCEPTION 'WPA_USER_PLAN_CHANGES_APPEND_ONLY' USING ERRCODE='55000';\nEND $$;\n\nDROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS wpa_user_plan_changes_immutable ON wpa_user_plan_changes;\nCREATE TRIGGER wpa_user_plan_changes_immutable\nBEFORE UPDATE OR DELETE ON wpa_user_plan_changes\nFOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION wpa_user_plan_changes_immutable();\n```","verify":"","src":"src/immutable_plan_ledger.sql"},{"slug":"stripe-webhook-environment-catalog-fencing","q":"How to prevent test-mode Stripe webhook replays and cross-account spoofing from granting unearned subscriptions?","problem":"Verifying Stripe webhook signatures using `stripe.webhooks.constructEvent()` only proves that the payload was signed by the configured webhook signing secret. In staging/production shared environments or Stripe Connect multi-account configurations, signing secrets can be misrouted. An attacker or staging tester sending a signed test-mode event (`livemode: false`) to a production webhook endpoint can trick the server into provisioning free paid subscriptions.","naive":[{"t":"Standard Signature Verification Alone","n":["Signature checks only validate cryptographic integrity against a signing secret. If a test webhook endpoint URL points to production, signatures validate successfully."]},{"t":"Missing Livemode Cross-Referencing","n":["Applications frequently check `event.type === 'checkout.session.completed'` and immediately grant tenant entitlements without validating whether `event.livemode` matches the server's configured Stripe API key."]},{"t":"Cross-Account Spoofing in Multi-Tenant Connect","n":["In multi-account architectures, webhooks originating from an unauthorized connected merchant account can be replayed against global platform handlers."]}],"arch":"1. **Cryptographic Key Prefix Inference:** `expectedStripeLiveMode()` inspects the server's configured secret key. If it begins with `sk_live_`, livemode must strictly be `true`; if `sk_test_`, livemode must be `false`.\n2. **Production Environment Assertion:** In `production`, if `expectedLiveMode` is unverified or `event.livemode` is missing, the request is immediately rejected with HTTP 503 `BILLING_PROVIDER_ENVIRONMENT_UNVERIFIED`.\n3. **Deterministic Mode Mismatch Fencing:** If `event.livemode !== expectedLiveMode`, the handler fails closed with HTTP 400 `BILLING_PROVIDER_ENVIRONMENT_MISMATCH`.\n4. **Stripe Connect Account Fencing:** Asserts that `event.account` matches the exact configured `catalog.accountId`, preventing multi-account cross-tenant replay attacks.\n\n```javascript\n// 1. Derive expected live mode from cryptographic secret key prefix\nfunction expectedStripeLiveMode(config) {\n    const secretKey = stripeCatalog(config).secretKey || '';\n    if (secretKey.startsWith('sk_live_')) return true;\n    if (secretKey.startsWith('sk_test_')) return false;\n    return null;\n}\n\n// 2. Strict webhook environment and account attestation\nfunction assertStripeWebhookEnvironment(event, config) {\n    const catalog = stripeCatalog(config);\n    const expectedLiveMode = expectedStripeLiveMode(config);\n    if (config.nodeEnv === 'production' && (expectedLiveMode === null || typeof event.livemode !== 'boolean')) {\n        throw new AppError('Stripe webhook environment could not be verified.', { status: 503, code: 'BILLING_PROVIDER_ENVIRONMENT_UNVERIFIED' });\n    }\n    if (typeof event.livemode === 'boolean' && expectedLiveMode !== null && event.livemode !== expectedLiveMode) {\n        throw new AppError('Stripe webhook environment does not match the configured key.', { status: 400, code: 'BILLING_PROVIDER_ENVIRONMENT_MISMATCH' });\n    }\n    if (catalog.accountId && event.account && catalog.accountId !== event.account) {\n        throw new AppError('Stripe webhook account does not match the configured account.', { status: 400, code: 'BILLING_PROVIDER_ACCOUNT_MISMATCH' });\n    }\n}\n```","verify":"","src":"src/stripe_webhook_fence.js"}],"dir":"07-fintech-and-billing-invariants","blurb":"Partial unique indexes, timing-safe webhooks, audit atomicity"},{"n":8,"name":"DevOps & Disaster Recovery","cases":[{"slug":"automated-ephemeral-database-restore-validation","q":"How to guarantee disaster recovery readiness by automatically verifying encrypted database restores in disposable containers?","problem":"\"Untested backups are not backups.\" Most development teams configure daily `pg_dump` cron jobs but never test restoring them until catastrophic production data loss occurs, only to discover corrupted archives, missing encryption keys, or schema migration incompatibility.","naive":[{"t":"Silent Backup Corruption","n":["Backups can complete with exit code 0 while producing truncated files or failing SHA-256 validation due to disk or network glitches."]},{"t":"Untested Encryption Keys","n":["Backups encrypted with modern public-key crypto (like `age`) often fail restore drills because private keys were rotated or lost."]},{"t":"Manual Disaster Recovery Drills","n":["Manual restore drills are done quarterly or never, leaving production vulnerable to silent data loss between drills."]}],"arch":"1. **Strict Shell Safety:** Runs with `set -eu` and `umask 077` to guarantee error termination and restrict temporary decrypted file permissions.\n2. **Integrity & Decryption Pipeline:** Automatically validates `sha256sum --check` and decrypts `age` encrypted backups into temporary RAM dumps.\n3. **Disposable Database Provisioning:** Generates an isolated ephemeral PostgreSQL database (`wpa_restore_check_${timestamp}`) and restores with `--exit-on-error`.\n4. **Automated Schema & Entity Assertions:** Queries row counts across core tables (`wpa_schema_migrations`, `wpa_workspaces`, `wpa_reports`) to assert semantic integrity.\n5. **Trap-Based Deterministic Cleanup:** POSIX shell `trap cleanup EXIT HUP INT TERM` ensures the temporary database and decrypted files are destroyed upon completion or failure, leaving no residual temporary files.","verify":"","src":"src/restore_check.sh"},{"slug":"production-runtime-drift-merkle-tree-verification","q":"How to verify container runtime drift and database migration checksums using SHA-256 Merkle trees?","problem":"Deploying container images without deterministic verification introduces \"runtime drift\": files modified directly inside containers by hot-fixes, missing configuration files, untracked development scripts, or tampered database migrations that diverge from checked-in Git repositories.","naive":[{"t":"Trusting Docker Image Tags (`:latest / :v1.0.0`)","n":["Tags are mutable and provide no cryptographic guarantee regarding what exact code is executing inside running containers."]},{"t":"Manual File Inspection (`docker exec diff`)","n":["Incomplete and error-prone; fails to account for `.dockerignore` exclusions, symlinks, or database schema migration ledgers."]},{"t":"Database Migration State Discrepancies","n":["Traditional migration runners check which version number ran, but never verify whether someone altered the SQL contents of past migrations."]}],"arch":"1. **Recursive SHA-256 Tree Hashing:** Traverses directory trees, computing deterministic SHA-256 hashes and byte counts for every deployable source and runtime file.\n2. **Rule-Based Exclusion Classification:** Integrates with `.dockerignore` rules (`test-code`, `coverage`, `local-environment`) so legitimate image boundaries are never falsely flagged as drift.\n3. **Database Ledger Checksum Verification:** Connects to PostgreSQL, compares `wpa_schema_migrations` SHA-256 hashes against actual migration files on disk, and flags tampering.\n4. **Read-Only Non-Destructive Audit:** Executes read-only analysis without modifying live container state.","verify":"","src":"src/runtime_drift.js"},{"slug":"durable-post-purge-audit-ledger-foreign-keys","q":"How to retain durable forensic proof of GDPR tenant deletions using ON DELETE SET NULL decoupling?","problem":"Under GDPR \"Right to be Forgotten\" and SOC 2 data retention mandates, deleting a customer organization requires purging all customer-owned data (reports, scanned repositories, source inputs). If the deletion tracking ledger references the workspace with standard `ON DELETE CASCADE`, deleting the workspace wipes the deletion execution row itself! This leaves administrators and auditors with no audit proof that the purge occurred, who authorized it, or how many artifacts were purged.","naive":[{"t":"Standard `ON DELETE CASCADE`","n":["Cascading foreign keys wipe the audit trail along with the customer records, destroying legal compliance records."]},{"t":"Application-Level Text Log Files","n":["Logging deletion runs to stdout or text files creates ephemeral records that can be rotated, lost, or lack cryptographic association with database transactions."]},{"t":"Soft-Delete \"IsDeleted\" Workarounds","n":["Soft-deleting records (`is_deleted = true`) violates strict GDPR mandates requiring complete physical erasure of customer PII and source code."]}],"arch":"1. **Foreign Key Decoupling via `ON DELETE SET NULL`:** By defining `workspace_id text REFERENCES wpa_workspaces(id) ON DELETE SET NULL`, the database engine permits the workspace record and all its private child tables to be permanently deleted via cascading purge.\n2. **Durable Forensic Counters:** The deletion run record survives the cascade. Its `workspace_id` gracefully transitions to `NULL`, while retaining:\n   * `request_id`: Unique transaction identifier.\n   * `actor_id`: Identity of the administrator or user who authorized the purge.\n   * Quantitative forensic counters: `deleted_reports`, `deleted_source_inputs`, `deleted_executions`.\n   * Execution timestamps: `started_at`, `completed_at`.\n3. **Status Indexing:** `ON wpa_workspace_deletion_runs(status, started_at)` provides fast querying for background retention workers checking stalled or failing purge runs.\n\n```sql\n-- 1. Durable execution history surviving workspace cascade for compliance audit\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS wpa_workspace_deletion_runs (\n    id text PRIMARY KEY,\n    request_id text NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n    workspace_id text REFERENCES wpa_workspaces(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,\n    status text NOT NULL CHECK (status IN ('running','completed','failed')),\n    attempts integer NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    actor_id text,\n    deleted_reports integer NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    deleted_source_inputs integer NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    deleted_executions integer NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n    failure_code text,\n    started_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n    completed_at timestamptz\n);\n\n-- 2. Performance indexes supporting retention status monitoring\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS wpa_workspace_deletion_runs_status_idx\n    ON wpa_workspace_deletion_runs(status, started_at);\n```","verify":"","src":"src/retention_audit_ledger.sql"},{"slug":"github-actions-fork-permission-decoupling","q":"How to decouple CI/CD sticky comment permissions to keep test pipelines green on external fork pull requests?","problem":"1. **Fork CI Pipeline Failure:** In open-source GitHub repositories, workflows triggered by external fork pull requests execute with read-only `GITHUB_TOKEN` permissions. Actions attempting to create or update sticky PR comments fail with HTTP 403 Forbidden, failing the entire CI workflow.\n2. **Blocked External Contributions:** Legitimate community pull requests fail automated build checks even if all unit tests pass, increasing maintainer review friction.\n3. **Security Risks of Elevated Permissions:** Giving `pull_request_target` elevated write permissions to run untrusted contributor code risks host secret exfiltration.","naive":[{"t":"Monolithic Workflow Steps","n":["Intertwining automated testing and PR comment reporting into an unconditioned step causes reporting errors to mask test outcomes."]},{"t":"Switching to `pull_request_target`","n":["Triggering workflows in the base repository context allows malicious fork code to access write permissions and secrets."]},{"t":"Ignoring Failures Globally","n":["Wrapping entire test jobs in `continue-on-error` masks real test failures."]}],"arch":"1. **Repository Ownership Fencing:** Privileged comment steps are guarded with `if: github.event.pull_request.head.repo.full_name == github.repository`, cleanly skipping comment publishing on fork pull requests.\n2. **Unconditional Artifact Preservation:** Generated Markdown summaries and `lcov.info` files are always uploaded via `actions/upload-artifact`, allowing maintainers to inspect coverage reports without elevated permissions.\n3. **Non-Gating Reporting:** Even on internal branches, comment steps use `continue-on-error: true`, ensuring transient GitHub API rate limits never fail green code merges.","verify":"","src":"src/fork_permission_pipeline.yml"},{"slug":"automated-shell-script-byte-level-lf-audit","q":"How to eliminate '/bin/sh^M: bad interpreter' container crashes with pre-flight byte-level LF unit tests?","problem":"In cross-platform engineering teams, developers working on Windows machines often edit bootstrap scripts, database backup scripts, or Docker entrypoint scripts. Windows editors default to CRLF (`\\r\\n`) line endings. When pushed and built into Debian, Alpine, or Ubuntu Docker images, executing the script fails with cryptic POSIX kernel errors: `/bin/sh: ./entrypoint.sh: /bin/sh^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory`, bringing down production deployments during pod startup.","naive":[{"t":"Relying on Developer Operating System Discipline","n":["Hoping all developers configure their local IDEs or git configurations with `core.autocrlf = input` is non-deterministic and fails on new developer machines."]},{"t":"Post-Build Dockerfile Workarounds (`dos2unix`)","n":["Running `RUN dos2unix /entrypoint.sh` in Dockerfiles adds container layer overhead and bloat to production base images."]},{"t":"Missing Automated CI Verification","n":["Linters typically check code syntax, ignoring raw byte-level carriage returns in untracked or auxiliary shell scripts."]}],"arch":"1. **Git-Aware Shell Discovery:** Executes `git ls-files -z` to discover all tracked files safely across null-terminated byte streams.\n2. **Byte-Level Shebang Inspection:** Evaluates raw buffer slices without string conversions. Checks for `.sh` extension or POSIX shebang syntax (`^#!.*(?:/|env\\s+)(?:ba|da|a|k|z)?sh`).\n3. **Strict Carriage Return Assertion:** Filters buffer bytes for `0x0d` (ASCII carriage return `\\r`). Any script containing even a single `\\r` immediately fails the test suite.\n4. **Git Attributes Enforcement:** Validates repository `.gitattributes` using `git check-attr eol`, ensuring git checkout rules enforce `eol=lf` permanently across all developer environments.\n\n```javascript\n// 1. Inspect raw buffer bytes for shebang lines and Windows CRLF carriage returns\nfunction isShellScript(relativePath, bytes) {\n    if (relativePath.endsWith('.sh')) return true;\n    const newline = bytes.indexOf(0x0a);\n    const firstLine = bytes.subarray(0, newline === -1 ? Math.min(bytes.length, 512) : newline).toString('utf8').replace(/\\r$/, '');\n    return /^#!.*(?:\\/|env\\s+)(?:ba|da|a|k|z)?sh(?:\\s|$)/.test(firstLine);\n}\n\n// 2. Automated test asserting no carriage return bytes (0x0d) across all tracked shell files\ntest('tracked Linux shell scripts use LF-only bytes and explicit Git attributes', () => {\n    const scripts = trackedShellScripts();\n    const carriageReturns = scripts.filter(({ bytes }) => bytes.includes(0x0d)).map(({ relativePath }) => relativePath);\n    assert.deepEqual(carriageReturns, [], `carriage-return bytes found in: ${carriageReturns.join(', ')}`);\n\n    const checkedAttributes = execFileSync('git', ['check-attr', 'eol', '--', ...scripts.map(s => s.relativePath)], { encoding: 'utf8' });\n    assert.match(checkedAttributes, /: eol: lf$/m);\n});\n```","verify":"","src":"src/shell_line_endings_audit.js"}],"dir":"08-devops-and-disaster-recovery","blurb":"Automated ephemeral database restore verification, retention ledgers"},{"n":9,"name":"Frontend Architecture & Observability","cases":[{"slug":"event-sourced-progress-reduction-state-machine","q":"How to eliminate out-of-order UI flickers in SSE streams using an event-sourced progress reduction state machine?","problem":"When multi-engine background workflows (running Lighthouse, YellowLab, Axe, ZAP) stream progress events over Server-Sent Events (SSE), network disconnects and packet jitter cause events to arrive out of order or re-deliver upon reconnection. Handling progress via ad-hoc mutable state variables in React (`setEngineAStatus`, `setEngineBStatus`) leads to race conditions, lost error badges, flickering UI transitions, and desynchronized progress bars.","naive":[{"t":"Ad-Hoc Mutable State Updates","n":["Updating individual component states on each incoming websocket/SSE message creates cascading React re-renders and out-of-order state corruption."]},{"t":"Reconnection Blindness","n":["When an SSE connection drops and reconnects with a fresh snapshot or backfilled event log, ad-hoc state cannot reconcile past and future progress deterministically."]},{"t":"Multi-Page Audit Desynchronization","n":["In multi-page website crawls, events from Page 1 and Page 2 interleave, corrupting single-page progress indicators."]}],"arch":"1. **Pure Event Reduction State Machine:** Treats UI state as a deterministic projection over an append-only `ScanProgressEvent[]` event log.\n2. **Reverse Event Folding:** Evaluates `[...snapshot.events].reverse().find(...)` to locate the latest state for each engine and page. Completed, failed, or retrying states naturally supersede prior queued states.\n3. **Execution Plan Alignment:** Matches active engines against the canonical `analysis.plan` payload, ensuring dynamic engine rosters (e.g. Lighthouse vs Axe) accurately reflect server-side dispatch plans.\n4. **Resilient Reconnect Invariant:** Because the UI state is a pure function of `(snapshot, events)`, SSE reconnects simply replay or append events, completely eliminating state flicker and race conditions.\n\n```typescript\n// 1. Deriving deterministic view models from an append-only event log\nfunction rowsFor(snapshot: ScanProgressSnapshot): { rows: EngineRow[]; page?: ScanProgressPage } {\n  const planEvent = latestPlan(snapshot.events);\n  const pageKey = planEvent?.payload?.pageKey;\n  const page = snapshot.pages.find((candidate) => pageKey && candidate.pageKey === pageKey)\n    || snapshot.pages.find((candidate) => [\"running\", \"retrying\"].includes(candidate.status))\n    || snapshot.pages.at(-1);\n\n  const plan = planEvent?.payload?.engines || [];\n  const definitions = plan.flatMap((execution) => (execution.engineIds || []).map((engineId) => ({\n    id: engineId,\n    resourceClass: execution.resourceClass,\n  })));\n\n  const seen = new Set<string>();\n  return {\n    page,\n    rows: definitions.filter((d) => !seen.has(d.id) && seen.add(d.id)).map((definition) => {\n      const executionEvent = [...snapshot.events].reverse().find((event) =>\n        event.type.startsWith(\"engine.\") &&\n        (!pageKey || event.payload?.pageKey === pageKey) &&\n        event.payload?.engineIds?.includes(definition.id)\n      );\n      return { id: definition.id, status: executionEvent?.payload?.status || \"queued\" };\n    }),\n  };\n}\n```","verify":"","src":"src/event_progress_reducer.ts"},{"slug":"zero-dependency-svg-coordinate-sparkline","q":"How to render 6-week trend sparklines in 13 lines of native SVG coordinate math with no charting dependencies?","problem":"Rendering simple six-week audit finding trends in web dashboards often tempts developers to pull in heavy charting dependencies (such as Chart.js, Recharts, or D3.js). These libraries add a significant amount of JavaScript bundle bloat, multiple canvas context lifecycles, and hydration layout shifts (CLS), negatively impacting Core Web Vitals for what is fundamentally a simple 6-point line graph.","naive":[{"t":"Client Bundle Inflation","n":["Adding Chart.js or Recharts increases first-contentful-paint (FCP) time and mobile memory usage."]},{"t":"Hydration Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS)","n":["Responsive charting libraries measure container DOM dimensions asynchronously via `ResizeObserver`, rendering empty canvas spaces during SSR and snapping after hydration."]},{"t":"Accessibility (a11y) Omission","n":["Canvas-rendered charts are opaque pixel grids that screen readers cannot inspect without custom ARIA fallback layers."]}],"arch":"1. **Pure ViewBox Vector Projection:** Computes SVG coordinates directly in 13 lines of TypeScript:\n   * Maps time intervals across the X-axis: `36 + index * (484 / (length - 1))`.\n   * Scales quantitative values on the inverted Y-axis: `210 - (count / max) * 160`.\n2. **Inline Polyline & Closed-Area Path Generation:**\n   * Joins line coordinates using SVG path syntax: `${index ? 'L' : 'M'}${x},${y}`.\n   * Closes the area under the curve to create a sleek gradient backdrop: `${line} L${endX},220 L36,220 Z`.\n3. **Fixed ViewBox Aspect Ratio:** Native SVG `viewBox=\"0 0 560 260\"` ensures the browser's layout engine reserves exact aspect ratio dimensions immediately during initial HTML parsing without client-side measurement.\n4. **Built-in Semantic Accessibility:** Implements `role=\"img\"` with an `aria-label` describing findings trends, making the chart fully screen-reader accessible.\n\n```typescript\n// 13-line zero-dependency SVG sparkline calculating polyline and area paths directly\nexport default function TrendChart({ data }: { data: Array<{ at: string; count: number }> }) {\n  const values = data.length ? data : Array.from({ length: 6 }, (_, index) => ({ at: new Date(Date.now() - (5 - index) * 604_800_000).toISOString(), count: 0 }));\n  const max = Math.max(5, ...values.map((point) => point.count));\n  const points = values.map((point, index) => ({ x: 36 + index * (484 / Math.max(values.length - 1, 1)), y: 210 - (point.count / max) * 160, ...point }));\n  const line = points.map((point, index) => `${index ? 'L' : 'M'}${point.x},${point.y}`).join(' ');\n  const area = `${line} L${points.at(-1)?.x || 520},220 L36,220 Z`;\n  return <div className=\"trend-chart\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 560 260\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Findings trend over six weeks\">\n    {[50, 90, 130, 170, 210].map((y) => <line key={y} x1=\"36\" y1={y} x2=\"530\" y2={y} className=\"trend-grid\" />)}\n    <path d={area} className=\"trend-area\" /><path d={line} className=\"trend-line\" />\n    {points.map((point) => <g key={point.at}><circle cx={point.x} cy={point.y} r=\"4.5\" /><text x={point.x} y=\"246\" textAnchor=\"middle\">{new Date(point.at).toLocaleDateString('en', { month: 'short', day: 'numeric' })}</text></g>)}\n  </svg><span><i /> Total findings</span></div>;\n}\n```","verify":"Run the component size measurement script using Node.js:\n\n```bash\nnode test/measure_size.js\n```\n\n### Verified Output:\n```text\n--- Zero-Dependency SVG Sparkline Size Measurement ---\nFile: src/TrendChart.tsx\nLines: 13 lines\nRaw Size: 1345 bytes (1.31 KB)\nGzip Size (level 9): 764 bytes (0.75 KB)\n```\n\n_Note: Measures the source file, not a compiled/minified bundle._\n\n* **Test Environment:** Node.js v24.17.0, Windows 11, 2026-10-17 (no external dependencies, native `node:zlib`)*","src":"src/TrendChart.tsx"},{"slug":"compiler-direct-dom-codegen","q":"How to bypass Virtual DOM diffing overhead by compiling reactive templates directly into imperative DOM operations?","problem":"Traditional Virtual DOM frameworks (like React) represent component templates as nested JavaScript objects (VDOM nodes). On every state update, the component re-executes, re-allocating tree structures in memory and traversing the entire hierarchy to diff changes. For large UI trees, this garbage collector churn and reconciliation overhead limits performance on low-power devices.","naive":[{"t":"Heap Allocation Overhead","n":["Generating thousands of ephemeral VNode objects on every keystroke forces frequent V8 garbage collection pauses."]},{"t":"Whole-Component Re-Execution","n":["State changes force entire component render functions to re-evaluate, including static subtrees."]},{"t":"Reconciliation Cost","n":["Comparing old and new virtual trees is O(N) relative to the component size, rather than O(1) relative to the changed state."]}],"arch":"1. **Compiler-First Transformation:** FluxDOM's template transformer compiles `.flow` components directly into imperative browser DOM calls (`createElement`, `createText`, `insert`).\n2. **Fine-Grained Attribute Effects:** Compiles dynamic bindings (`:class=\"active ? 'on' : 'off'\"`) into isolated `createEffect` closures. When `active` changes, only the single `setAttribute` call executes; no parent or child elements are touched.\n3. **Surgical Text Node Mutation:** Reactive text interpolations (`{count}`) compile into an initial `createText('')` and an effect updating `text.nodeValue`.\n4. **Diffing-Free Runtime:** Eliminates Virtual DOM tree diffing entirely, dispatching state changes directly to fine-grained DOM mutation effects.\n\n```typescript\n// 1. Compiling static elements into direct DOM creation operations\nconst el = createElement('ul');\nsetAttribute(el, 'data-fluxdom', scopeHash);\naddEventListener(el, 'click', handleClick);\n\n// 2. Compiling dynamic bindings into targeted fine-grained effects\ncreateEffect(() => {\n  setAttribute(el, 'class', __active() ? 'on' : 'off');\n});\n\n// 3. Compiling reactive text interpolation directly to Text node mutations\nconst expr = createText('');\ncreateEffect(() => {\n  setText(expr, String(__count()));\n});\ninsert(el, expr);\n```","verify":"","src":"src/direct_dom_codegen.ts"},{"slug":"static-ast-render-strategy-resolution","q":"How to automatically resolve SSG, SSR, and CSR rendering strategies using ahead-of-time static AST inspection?","problem":"In modern full-stack web applications, developers must manually annotate which pages or components should be statically generated (SSG), server-rendered (SSR), or client-rendered (CSR). Human error frequently leads to pages with client-only APIs (`window`, `localStorage`) failing during SSR builds, or purely static marketing pages wasting server compute because they were unnecessarily configured for dynamic SSR.","naive":[{"t":"Manual Framework Directives (`use client` / `ssr: false`)","n":["Relies on developer memory and discipline; missing an annotation causes hydration crashes in production."]},{"t":"Runtime Detection Penalties","n":["Detecting client capabilities at runtime introduces layout shifts (CLS) and extra loading spinners."]},{"t":"Unnecessary Server Render Costs","n":["Rendering static pages on every HTTP request wastes server memory and increases Time-to-First-Byte (TTFB)."]}],"arch":"1. **AST AST Analysis Pass:** `analyze()` evaluates both script declarations and template nodes:\n   * Detects reactive signal declarations (`let count = 0`).\n   * Detects asynchronous operations (`await`, `fetch()`).\n   * Detects browser side-effects (`window.`, `document.`, `localStorage`).\n2. **Template Directive Traversal:** Inspects nodes for dynamic bindings (`:class`), event listeners (`@click`), and structural directives (`{#if}`, `{#each}`).\n3. **Deterministic Strategy Classification:**\n   * If browser globals or event listeners exist -> Automatically assigns `CSR`.\n   * If async calls, signals, or bindings exist -> Automatically assigns `SSR`.\n   * If no signals, no events, and no side-effects exist -> Automatically promotes to `SSG` (Static Site Generation).\n4. **Metadata Code Generation:** Emits `export const __flowStrategy = 'SSG';` directly into the compiled output for build tool integration.\n\n```typescript\n// 1. Static inspection of script and template AST for reactivity markers\nconst signals = detectSignals(scriptContent);\nconst hasAsync = /await\\s|fetch\\(/.test(scriptContent);\nconst hasSideEffects = /window\\.|document\\.|localStorage/.test(scriptContent);\n\nfor (const node of component.template?.children ?? []) {\n  analyzeTemplateNode(node, templateAnalysis);\n}\n\n// 2. Deterministic rendering strategy classification\nlet strategy: RenderStrategy = 'SSG';\nif (hasSideEffects || templateAnalysis.hasEvents) {\n  strategy = 'CSR';\n} else if (\n  hasAsync ||\n  signals.size > 0 ||\n  templateAnalysis.hasBindings ||\n  templateAnalysis.hasDirectives ||\n  templateAnalysis.hasExpressions\n) {\n  strategy = 'SSR';\n}\n```","verify":"","src":"src/strategy_resolver.ts"},{"slug":"scoped-css-pseudo-class-splicing","q":"How to preserve pseudo-class specificity and prevent CSS animation corruption during scoped component style compilation?","problem":"Scoped CSS in Single File Components ensures component styles do not leak to other parts of the application. However, naive scoped CSS compilers that simply append attribute selectors to the end of rules (e.g. `button:hover[data-scope]`) corrupt CSS semantics:\n1. Attaching attributes after pseudo-classes or pseudo-elements (`button::before[data-scope]`) produces invalid CSS that browsers reject entirely.\n2. Transforming keyframe percentage rules (`0% { opacity: 0 }` -> `0%[data-scope]`) breaks CSS animations.","naive":[{"t":"End-of-String Appending","n":["`selector + scope` fails on `:hover`, `:focus`, `:disabled`, and pseudo-elements like `::before`."]},{"t":"Animation Keyframe Corruption","n":["Scoping `from`, `to`, and `50%` selectors inside `@keyframes` blocks breaks animations."]},{"t":"Media Query & At-Rule Mangling","n":["Naive regex parsers attempt to scope `@media (min-width: 768px)`, producing broken stylesheet syntax."]}],"arch":"1. **Pseudo-Class Boundary Detection:** Scans selector strings with `selector.search(/:{1,2}[A-Za-z-])` to find the exact index of any pseudo-class or pseudo-element.\n2. **Pre-Pseudo Splicing:** Splices `[data-fluxdom=\"hash\"]` *before* the colon (`button[data-fluxdom]:hover`). This preserves browser selector matching and CSS specificity rules.\n3. **Keyframe Step Whitelist:** `isKeyframeSelector()` checks for `from`, `to`, and `^\\d+(?:\\.\\d+)?%$`, leaving animation keyframe steps untouched.\n4. **At-Rule Pass-Through:** Ignores selectors starting with `@` (`@media`, `@supports`), scoping only inner declarations.\n\n```typescript\n// 1. Scope selector transformation attaching attribute before pseudo-classes\nfunction scopeSelector(selector: string, scope: string): string {\n  if (selector === ':root' || selector.includes(':global(')) return selector;\n  const pseudoIndex = selector.search(/:{1,2}[A-Za-z-]/);\n  if (pseudoIndex < 0) return `${selector}${scope}`;\n  return `${selector.slice(0, pseudoIndex)}${scope}${selector.slice(pseudoIndex)}`;\n}\n\n// 2. Preserving CSS at-rules and keyframe step selectors untouched\nfunction isKeyframeSelector(selector: string): boolean {\n  return selector === 'from' || selector === 'to' || /^\\d+(?:\\.\\d+)?%$/.test(selector);\n}\n\nexport function transformStyle(styleContent: string, hash: string): string {\n  if (!styleContent) return '';\n  const scope = `[data-fluxdom=\"${hash}\"]`;\n  return styleContent.replace(/([^{}]+)\\{/g, (full, rawSelector: string) => {\n    const selector = rawSelector.trim();\n    if (!selector || selector.startsWith('@') || isKeyframeSelector(selector)) return full;\n    const scoped = selector.split(',').map((part) => scopeSelector(part.trim(), scope)).join(', ');\n    return `${rawSelector.match(/^\\s*/)?.[0] ?? ''}${scoped} {`;\n  });\n}\n```","verify":"Run the automated test suite using Node.js built-in test runner with native TypeScript type stripping (requires Node 22.6+):\n\n```bash\nnode --experimental-strip-types --test test/scoped_css_transformer.test.ts\n```\n\n### Verified Output:\n```text\n✔ splices scoping attribute BEFORE pseudo-classes and pseudo-elements (:hover, ::before) (1.14ms)\n✔ preserves keyframe steps (from, to, percentage) and @-rules without mangling selectors (0.24ms)\n✔ preserves :root and :global(...) selectors without scope injection (0.17ms)\nℹ tests 3\nℹ suites 0\nℹ pass 3\nℹ fail 0\n```\n\n* **Test Environment:** Node.js v24.17.0, Windows 11, 2026-10-17 (no external dependencies, native type stripping)*","src":"src/scoped_css_transformer.ts"},{"slug":"reactive-time-travel-tracking-suspension","q":"How to implement non-reactive time-travel undo/redo in reactive stores without triggering recursive state corruption?","problem":"1. **Infinite Undo Recursion:** In reactive stores, restoring a historical snapshot writes to reactive signals. These writes fire active effects, which push the restored state as a brand new forward action, immediately wiping the redo stack.\n2. **Intermediate Micro-Render Thrashing:** Restoring an object with 20 properties triggers 20 distinct effect executions and DOM re-renders unless wrapped in an atomic batch.\n3. **Unbounded Memory Retention:** Recording entire state snapshots on every keystroke without depth bounds causes continuous V8 heap memory growth.","naive":[{"t":"Unflagged State Restoration","n":["Treating undo operations identically to user actions destroys the historical past/future timeline."]},{"t":"Unbatched Property Assignment","n":["Looping through properties and setting signals individually causes layout thrashing and intermediate broken states."]},{"t":"Naive Reference Stacking","n":["Pushing object references without deep serialization clones mutates past snapshots when current state changes."]}],"arch":"1. **Tracking Suspension Fence (`isTracking = false`):** Before applying a historical snapshot, history observation is suspended. The state update occurs inside a `batch()` block, guaranteeing a single atomic reflow, and tracking is restored in a `finally` clause.\n2. **Bounded FIFO Stack (`maxHistory` Clamping):** Past states are clamped using `past.shift()`, keeping memory usage strictly O(1) regardless of session duration.\n3. **State Equality Short-Circuiting:** Consecutive identical states are discarded via JSON serialization equality checks before stack pushing.","verify":"","src":"src/history_controller.ts"},{"slug":"directional-graph-link-endpoint-preservation","q":"How to preserve directional relation link endpoints when splitting association class nodes in graph layout engines?","problem":"1. **Inverted Graphviz Visual Geometry:** In PlantUML object diagrams, declaring a directional relation with an association node (`chases -down-> dog` and `(chases, dog) . annotation`) inverted the visual layout arrow, pointing upwards instead of downwards.\n2. **Double Inversion Normalization:** When creating association class nodes, the parser attempts to normalize entity order logically. Checking `isInverted()` when the link already carries directional layout metadata causes a redundant second inversion.\n3. **Graph Layout Desynchronization:** Directional hints (`-down->`, `-right->`, `-up->`, `-left->`) in UML diagrams are essential for readability and architecture hierarchy; flipping arrow directions corrupts model semantics.","naive":[{"t":"Blind Entity Order Normalization","n":["Assuming that association classes must always bind to alphabetical or declaration entity order discards directional layout hints."]},{"t":"Post-Processing Graphviz Hacks","n":["Attempting to invert arrowheads in post-processing generates disjointed edge lines and broken routing curves."]},{"t":"Splitting Links without Endpoint Preservation","n":["Creating two link halves from an association point requires strictly inheriting the original link's directional vector."]}],"arch":"1. **Visual Endpoint Order Preservation:** In `AbstractClassOrObjectDiagram.createNew()`, the redundant ternary inversion check (`existingLink.isInverted() ? entity2 : entity1`) was eliminated.\n2. **Direct Entity Binding:** `entity1real` and `entity2real` are extracted directly via `existingLink.getEntity1()` and `existingLink.getEntity2()`, preserving the exact visual vector of the directional relation.\n3. **Golden Snapshot Testing:** Validated via automated Vega/Smetana regression tests across all 4 cardinal directions (up, down, left, right).","verify":"","src":"src/association_link_normalizer.java"},{"slug":"in-memory-virtual-css-vite-module-resolution","q":"How to feed scoped SFC CSS directly into Vite pipelines without temporary disk files or HMR watcher loops?","problem":"Compiling Single File Components containing `<style scoped>` blocks requires extracting CSS and feeding it into modern bundlers (like Vite, PostCSS, or Tailwind). Naive plugins write temporary CSS files to disk (e.g. `.tmp/component.css`), causing disk I/O bottlenecks, file watcher loops during Hot Module Replacement (HMR), and file system race conditions across concurrent worker threads.","naive":[{"t":"Disk I/O Bottlenecks","n":["Writing intermediate CSS files to `.tmp` on every keystroke wears SSDs and slows HMR feedback loops."]},{"t":"File Watcher Thrashing","n":["Writing temporary files triggers Vite's internal file system watcher, creating infinite build loops."]},{"t":"Dirty Working Trees","n":["Residual temporary files pollute source directories and risk accidental git commits."]}],"arch":"1. **In-Memory Buffer Cache:** Holds compiled, scoped CSS strings in a process-level `Map<string, string>()` keyed by the component's unique file ID.\n2. **Rollup Virtual Module Convention (`\\0`):** `resolveId()` prefixes virtual CSS identifiers with a null byte (`\\0${id}`). This tells Rollup and Vite that the module is a virtual asset not present on the physical disk.\n3. **Seamless Vite Pipeline Forwarding:** In `transform()`, appending `import \"${id}.css\";` instructs Vite to process the virtual module through its native CSS pipeline (PostCSS, CSS modules, autoprefixer, minifier) entirely in RAM.\n4. **HMR Coordination:** Sends full-reload WebSocket signals directly to `server.ws` when `.flow` components change.\n\n```typescript\n// 1. In-memory virtual CSS storage avoiding temporary disk file writes\nconst cssMap = new Map<string, string>();\n\nreturn {\n  name: 'vite-plugin-FluxDOM',\n  enforce: 'pre',\n\n  // 2. Resolve virtual CSS identifiers using Rollup null-byte convention\n  resolveId(id) {\n    if (id.endsWith('.flow.css')) return '\\0' + id;\n    return null;\n  },\n\n  // 3. Load virtual CSS directly from memory\n  load(id) {\n    if (id.startsWith('\\0') && id.endsWith('.flow.css')) {\n      const actualId = id.slice(1).replace('.css', '');\n      return cssMap.get(actualId) || '';\n    }\n    return null;\n  },\n\n  // 4. Transform .flow SFC: extract scoped CSS and inject virtual import\n  transform(code, id) {\n    if (!id.endsWith('.flow')) return null;\n    const { js, css } = compileSource(code, id);\n    if (css) cssMap.set(id, css);\n\n    return {\n      code: css ? `${js}\\nimport ${JSON.stringify(`${id}.css`)};` : js,\n      map: null,\n    };\n  }\n};\n```","verify":"","src":"src/virtual_css_plugin.ts"}],"dir":"09-frontend-architecture-and-observability","blurb":"Event-sourced state machines, zero-dependency SVG sparklines"}];
